var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"#BnGsim.jl","page":"Home","text":"BnGsim.jl simulates breeding programs in the JuliaBnG ecosystem. Version 0.4 runs discrete-generation breeding programs and provides the building blocks:\n\nFounders: Wright–Fisher burn-in through FisherWright.jl (historical_population, continue_population), founder sampling (founder_haplotypes, sample_founder_population), and readers and writers for packed haplotype files.\nKeyed streams: SimKey addresses every random stream by its coordinate in the simulation, so results do not depend on thread count or call order (keyed_rng).\nMeiosis: MeiosisMap and make_progeny! (keyed, word-wise, with per-chromosome genetic lengths and optional crossover records); the older gamete!/gene_drop! with an explicit rng; random_mate, rnd_ped.\nBreeding loop: founder_population, Scheme and simulate!: phenotypes, evaluation through Breeding.jl (true values, phenotype, PBLUP, GBLUP), selection by truncation, optimum contributions (TM1997, rocs) or at random, keyed mating and meiosis, with per-generation summaries; discrete or overlapping generations, several traits with selection on an index of multi-trait EBVs, sex- and age-limited records, survival and culling, single-step GBLUP, optional REML re-estimation of the variance components, and checkpoints.\nTraits: QTL sampling (sample_loci, eqtl, which keeps the shape of the effect distribution and sets the mean through the intercept), trait_summary, true breeding values (tbv!), and phenotypes with correlated residuals, sex-limited expression and threshold traits (phenotype!).\nDiagnostics and provenance: founder_summary, ld_decay, vanraden_g, provenance.\n\nEvery stochastic function takes an explicit rng. See the Roadmap for the planned scenario, evaluation and selection layers.","title":"BnGsim.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#A-breeding-program","page":"Home","text":"using BnGStructs, BnGsim, Random\n\nsp   = GenericSpecies(\"toy\", [20_000_000, 10_000_000])\nrng  = Xoshiro(1)\nvm   = VariantMap(sort(unique(rand(rng, 1:30_000_000, 2000))), sp)\nhap  = Haplotype(BitMatrix(rand(rng, Bool, length(vm), 400)))      # 200 founders\nt    = Trait(\"y\"; h² = 0.3, σₐ = 1.0)\nqtl  = eqtl(hap, 1:5:length(vm), t; rng = rng)\npop  = founder_population(hap, qtl, t, SimKey(2026))\nsc   = Scheme(; noff = 200, nsire = 10, ndam = 50, evaluation = :pblup)\nsimulate!(pop, sc, MeiosisMap(sp, vm), 10)    # one row per generation","title":"A breeding program"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"BnGsim requires Julia 1.10 (LTS) or later.\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.add(\"BnGsim\")","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Quick-start","page":"Home","text":"using BnGStructs, BnGsim, Random\n\nrng = Xoshiro(2026)\nfp  = sample_founder_population(Cattle(); ne = 100, n_id = 20, n_marker = 500, n_qtl = 50,\n                                h2 = 0.3, burnin_generations = 200, rng = rng)\nfp.tbv           # true breeding values of the founders\nfp.markers.loci  # marker chip loci in fp.map","title":"Quick start"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#API-Reference","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"API Reference"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BnGsim.FounderPopulation","page":"API Reference","text":"struct FounderPopulation\n\nHolds the complete state of a sampled founder population for breeding simulations.\n\nFields\n\nspecies::Species: Biological species definition (e.g. Cattle, Pig).\nhaplotype::Haplotype: Base genome haplotypes (nlc × 2nid).\nmap::VariantMap: Variant genomic map (VariantMap from BnGStructs).\nmarkers::LocusSet: Marker chip panel (LocusSet from BnGStructs).\nqtl::TraitQTL: Single-trait QTL architecture.\nh2::Float64: Target heritability.\ntbv::Vector{Float64}: True Breeding Values for the n founder individuals.\nphenotypes::Vector{Float64}: Simulated phenotypes (y = TBV + e).\npedigree::DataFrame: Pedigree with columns :id, :sire, :dam (sire/dam = 0 for founders).\n\nNote: fp.marker_indices is also supported as a property alias returning fp.markers.loci.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.FounderPopulation"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BnGsim.HistoricalSpec","page":"API Reference","text":"struct HistoricalSpec\n\nA historical (pre-founder) population run with FisherWright.fisher_wright.\n\nFields\n\nname::String: arm label, e.g. \"F2\".\nne::Int: diploid population size, constant over the run.\nnt::Int: number of discrete, non-overlapping generations.\nmr::Float64: mutations per 1e8 bp per meiosis (μ = mr × 1e-8).\nchromosome::Vector{UInt32}: autosome lengths in bp.\nM::Float64: bp per Morgan (1e8 is 1 cM/Mb).\n\nModel, as implemented by FisherWright with its neutral defaults: diploid, dioecious Wright-Fisher population with random union of gametes; infinite-sites mutation; Poisson crossovers without interference; independent assortment of chromosomes; the genome starts monomorphic, so nt determines how close the run is to mutation-drift equilibrium (about 10ne generations for the frequency spectrum, much less for LD at short distances).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.HistoricalSpec"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim.HistoricalSpec-Tuple{AbstractString, BnGStructs.Species, Integer, Integer, Real}","page":"API Reference","text":"HistoricalSpec(name, sp::Species, ne, nt, mr)\n\nThe run of ne diploid individuals for nt generations on the genome of sp (its chromosome lengths and bp per Morgan). The size is an argument: a species carries none since BnGStructs 0.4.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.HistoricalSpec"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BnGsim.MeiosisMap","page":"API Reference","text":"MeiosisMap(species, map::VariantMap)\n\nThe genome layout used by make_progeny!, built once: for every chromosome its physical start and end (genome-wide bp), its genetic length in Morgans (from BnGStructs.map_length), and the range of loci of map on it. The map is checked against the species with BnGStructs.check_coordinates.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.MeiosisMap"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BnGsim.MeiosisRecord","page":"API Reference","text":"MeiosisRecord\n\nCrossovers of the gametes made by one call of make_progeny!. Gamete g is column g of the offspring Haplotype (2i-1 from the sire, 2i from the dam of mating i).\n\nparent[g]: the parent (column of the mating plan) that made it;\nphase[c, g]: true if chromosome c starts on the parent's second haplotype;\ncrossovers(rec, g): genome-wide positions of its crossovers, in increasing order within each chromosome.\n\nWith the parental records this determines the founder origin of every segment, the input of segment-level IBD.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.MeiosisRecord"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BnGsim.PedigreeView","page":"API Reference","text":"PedigreeView(sire, dam, ids)\n\nA pedigree restricted to the animals ids, in the form RelationshipMatrices expects: dense rows 1:length(ids), parents before offspring, and 0 for an unknown parent. Row r is the animal v.ids[r]; its parents are v.sire[r] and v.dam[r], as rows, 0 when the parent is unknown or not in the view.\n\nsire and dam are the full pedigree by id (0 unknown), with every parent's id below its offspring's, as in a Population; ids need not be sorted or unique and are stored in ascending order, which is then a topological order. The view is the boundary between the domain ids, which are never reused, and the row indices of the relationship matrices and mixed-model equations built on it: a matrix built from v.sire and v.dam is indexed by rows, and row_to_id and id_to_row translate.\n\nSee also pedigree_view.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.PedigreeView"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BnGsim.Population","page":"API Reference","text":"Population\n\nAnimals of a breeding program, one row per animal in order of birth. Animal ids are 1:n and equal the row; nothing is ever removed, so the equality holds (invariant I4 holds trivially). Tables that leave the population carry the id explicitly (pedigree), and relationship matrices on a subset are indexed through a PedigreeView.\n\nFields:\n\nsire, dam (0 = unknown), male, generation (the birth cohort, i.e. the time step of birth; founders are 0; with discrete generations it is the generation number), exit (the first time step at which the animal is dead or culled; typemax(Int32) while it lives);\ntbv, phenotype, ebv: n × k matrices, one column per trait, of genetic values, phenotypes (NaN in the sex that does not express the trait) and the last evaluation;\nblocks, first, slot: the haplotypes, one Haplotype per cohort (nothing once no genome of the cohort is kept), with first[t + 1] the id of the first animal of cohort t and slot[i] the position of animal i in its cohort's block (0 once its genome is dropped, see retain in Scheme); read genomes with genomes;\nqtl (one TraitQTL per trait), traits, Lr (lower-triangular factor of the true residual covariance matrix, R = L_rL_r), key;\nG0, R0: the genetic and residual covariance matrices of the next evaluation (the true ones of the founders unless re-estimated);\nmeioses: the crossover records of each cohort (meioses[t] for cohort t ≥ 1, parents as population ids) when the scheme records them, nothing otherwise; see founder_segments.\n\nBuild one with founder_population, extend it with step!, and save or restore it with save_checkpoint and load_checkpoint.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.Population"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BnGsim.RandomSign","page":"API Reference","text":"RandomSign(d)\n\nEffects drawn from the distribution d with a random sign: x from d, then -x with probability ½ (one draw from d, then one Bool, per effect). With a Gamma d, the effect distribution of Meuwissen, Hayes and Goddard (2001): symmetric, with the heavy tails of the Gamma. Used as a Trait's da (scenario family \"gamma\" with \"sign\": \"random\"). Only rand, mean (0) and var (Ex^2 of d) are defined.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.RandomSign"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BnGsim.Scenario","page":"API Reference","text":"Scenario\n\nA validated simulation scenario, read from JSON with read_scenario or parse_scenario, written with write_scenario, and run with run_scenario. Every optional value is filled with its default, so the canonical JSON (scenario_json) states the whole simulation. Two scenarios are equal when all their values are.\n\nThe JSON document (schema \"bngsim-scenario\", version 1) has these keys; those without a default are required.\n\nTop level\n\nschema: \"bngsim-scenario\"; schema_version: 1;\nname: a label;\nseed: the master seed, an integer in 0:2^64-1 (every random stream derives from it through SimKey);\nreplicates (1): replicates 1:replicates;\nsteps: time steps of the breeding scheme.\n\nspecies either {\"preset\": \"Cattle\"} (one of Cattle, Sheep, Goat, Pig, Horse, Dog, Cat, Chicken, Rabbit), or {\"name\", \"chromosome\"} with autosome lengths in bp; and either M, bp per Morgan (1e8), or morgan, the genetic length of each chromosome. The canonical form lists name, chromosome and morgan.\n\nfounders: the historical population, run with FisherWright from no variation: ne diploids for nt generations at mr mutations per 1e8 bp per meiosis (1.0); then decline ([]), the census sizes of further generations (e.g. a decline to a breed); and nid, the founders sampled from the last generation. With shared_history (true) one historical population serves every replicate, each sampling its own founders; otherwise each replicate runs its own.\n\nloci: qtl, {\"nlc\", \"maf\"} (maf 0.0: loci with minor allele frequency above maf among the founders); chip (null), the same for the genotyping panel, needed by :gblup and :ssgblup; overlap (\"disjoint\"): whether chip loci may also be QTL (\"allowed\").\n\ntraits: a non-empty array of {\"name\", \"h2\" (0.25), \"sigma_a\" (1.0), \"mu\" (0.0), \"sex\" (2), \"age\" (0), \"da\"}, as BnGStructs.Trait: sex 0 females, 1 males, 2 both; age the whole time step of the record; da the distribution of the QTL effects before scaling, {\"family\": \"normal\", \"mean\": 0.0, \"sd\": 1.0} (default), {\"family\": \"gamma\", \"shape\", \"scale\": 1.0, \"sign\": \"positive\"} (\"sign\": \"random\" for effects of either sign, RandomSign), {\"family\": \"laplace\", \"scale\": 1.0} (location 0) or {\"family\": \"spike_slab\", \"pi\", \"sd\": 1.0, \"spike_sd\": 0.0} (SpikeSlab: a fraction pi of the effects from Normal(0, sd), the others 0, or from Normal(0, spike_sd)). Effects are scaled to sigma_a afterwards, so only the shape of the distribution matters; with several traits, only the first keeps it (see eqtl).\n\ngenetic_correlation, residual_correlation (identity): k × k correlation matrices of k traits, as arrays of rows.\n\nscheme: the keywords of Scheme, in ASCII: noff, nsire, ndam, evaluation, selection, K, lambda, kappa, ub, blend, max_sire_age, max_dam_age, reml, weights, survival, cull_unselected, genotyped_cohorts, ocs, delta_F, record_meiosis, window, ancestor_generations, retain, report_reliability, with the same defaults; null for K and delta_F (unset) and for ancestor_generations (all). The chip loci come from loci.chip.\n\nUnknown keys, duplicate keys and values of the wrong type are errors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.Scenario"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BnGsim.ScenarioError","page":"API Reference","text":"ScenarioError(errors)\n\nA scenario that cannot be read or does not validate. errors lists every problem found, each prefixed by its path in the JSON document (for example scheme.nsire).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.ScenarioError"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BnGsim.ScenarioSize","page":"API Reference","text":"ScenarioSize\n\nThe quantities of a scenario that its memory and time grow with, computed before it runs:\n\nalleles: the expected number of derived alleles a haplotype of the historical population carries, μL min(t 4N) for N = ne over t generations (nt plus the decline): μL new ones per generation, until fixation balances them at the neutral equilibrium 4NμL;\nloci: the expected number of loci segregating among the 2nid founder haplotypes, 4NμL ln(1 + 2n_id t  4N), the total branch length of their genealogy truncated at the start of the run (the population starts without variation);\nanimals: founders and offspring of the run, nid + steps × noff;\nevaluated: the largest pedigree evaluated, nid + (steps - 1) × noff;\ngenotyped: the animals in the dense relationship matrices of :gblup (all evaluated) and :ssgblup (the last genotyped_cohorts cohorts, or all); 0 otherwise;\ncohort: the largest cohort (the per-step summary forms its dense relationship matrix).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.ScenarioSize"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BnGsim.Scheme","page":"API Reference","text":"Scheme(; noff, nsire, ndam, evaluation = :pblup, selection = :truncation,\n       K = NaN, λ = 0.0, κ = 0.0, ub = 1.0, chip = Int32[], blend = 0.05,\n       max_sire_age = 0, max_dam_age = 0, reml = false, weights = Float64[],\n       survival = Float64[], cull_unselected = false, genotyped_cohorts = 0,\n       ocs = :fixed, ΔF = NaN, record_meiosis = false, report_reliability = false)\n\nA selection and mating scheme, applied every time step:\n\nnoff: offspring per step (one per mating);\nnsire, ndam: parents selected by truncation, and the number of each sex used by random selection;\nevaluation: :tbv (true values, an oracle), :phenotype (own records), :pblup (animal model with the pedigree relationship matrix), :gblup (animal model with G^* = (1-w)G + wA, w = blend, G VanRaden at the loci chip with base allele frequencies from the founders), :ssgblup (single-step, H^-1 of Legarra et al. 2009 and Christensen & Lund 2010 from RelationshipMatrices.hinv, with the animals of the last genotyped_cohorts cohorts genotyped at the loci chip (0: all animals), G as for :gblup and blended with A_22 by blend; with every animal genotyped it equals :gblup). With several traits the animal model is multi-trait, with an intercept per trait and residuals correlated within animals;\nweights: the index weights w (one per trait; empty: all 1). Candidates are ranked on I = what a, which with economic values as weights and multi-trait BLUP EBVs is the optimal index for the aggregate genotype H = wg (Henderson 1963). With :phenotype it is wy, a fixed-weight index, not the optimal phenotypic index;\nselection: :truncation (best nsire males and ndam females on the index, equal numbers of offspring), :ocs (optimum contributions; see ocs below), :rocs (Breeding.rocs with λ, κ, the prediction error covariance of the candidates' index as Ω, and an upper bound ub on each parent's contribution, in rocs units where each sex contributes ½; ub = 0.5/nsire with λ = κ = 0 is truncation selection), or :random (nsire and ndam chosen at random, a neutral reference);\nmax_sire_age, max_dam_age: the oldest age, in time steps, at which an animal can be selected as a parent (age 0: born in the current step). 0 for both gives discrete generations; larger values give overlapping generations, with all eligible ages competing on one criterion. With :ocs and :rocs the contributions are those of the candidates to the next cohort, without the age-class weighting of Meuwissen & Sonesson (1998) or Bijma & Woolliams (2000); the constraint then does not bound the long-term rate of inbreeding exactly;\nreml: if true, the covariance components of :pblup and :gblup are re-estimated by AI-REML (Breeding.jl) from all records before every evaluation, starting from the previous estimates, once every trait has a record on an animal with a known parent (before that, as with the founders' records alone, genetic and residual components are not separable and the true values are used); otherwise the true values of the founders are used. With all records and the full pedigree, REML estimates the base-population components even under selection (Sorensen & Kennedy 1984; infinitesimal model). Several traits are estimated with AI steps on the Cholesky factors (param = :cholesky of Breeding.reml), which keeps the estimates positive definite; early estimates may lie on the boundary (e.g. a genetic correlation near ±1 after one cohort);\nocs: the constraint of :ocs. :fixed: Breeding.TM1997 with cAc le K at every step (average coancestry K2). The others hold the rate of inbreeding at the target ΔF per generation (see ocs_contributions): :cohort constrains the mean kinship of the newborn cohort; :age_year the mean kinship of the population at the next step, age classes weighted by their remaining expected contributions (Meuwissen & Sonesson 1998; optiSel), with the per-step increase set by the estimated generation interval; :age_generation the same with the generation interval of the contributions being chosen (a per-generation constraint, Sonesson et al. 2000). With discrete generations the three coincide;\nrecord_meiosis: keep the crossover records of every cohort in pop.meioses, for realized IBD from founder segments (founder_segments); about 8 bytes per crossover;\nreport_reliability: report the mean reliability of the candidates of each step from the prediction error variances of the evaluation (:pblup, :gblup, :ssgblup; see step!). It costs a solve of the mixed-model equations per candidate and trait, so it is off by default (the column is then NaN), and the resource estimate does not include it (on 5500 animals and 10 steps of PBLUP it took 0.3 s more than the 0.4 s of the run);\nsurvival: survival[a + 1] is the probability that an animal alive at age a survives to age a + 1 (the last entry for all older ages; empty: no deaths). Deaths are drawn between steps (Domain.SURVIVAL), independently of genotype and records (missing at random), so they bias neither BLUP nor REML;\ncull_unselected: if true, eligible animals of age 1 or more that are not selected as parents at a step are culled. Young candidates (age 0) are kept for the next step. Culling depends on the EBV, so the animals left are a selected sample; BLUP and REML with all records and the pedigree account for that;\nwindow: if positive, :pblup, :gblup and :ssgblup use the records of the animals born in the last window time steps only (the current cohort and the window - 1 before it), on the pedigree of these animals and their ancestors traced for ancestor_generations generations (a PedigreeView); the other animals get no EBV (NaN). It must exceed the maximum ages, so that every candidate is evaluated. 0 (default): all records and the whole pedigree. Under selection, discarding the records of earlier cohorts and the parents beyond the traced depth biases BLUP (Henderson 1975), so a window trades accuracy for the size of the equations; REML with reml = true is then estimated from the window's records too;\nancestor_generations: generations of ancestors traced above the animals of the window (default: all; then the relationships among the window's animals are exact, and only records are discarded);\nretain: :all (default) keeps every genome. :active drops, after each step, the genomes no later step can use: those of animals that can no longer be parents (dead, culled, or past the maximum age of their sex) and are not genotyped by :ssgblup (outside the last genotyped_cohorts cohorts). The founders are kept (the base allele frequencies). Pedigree and records are kept for every animal; memory then grows with the animals that are still candidates, not with all animals born. Not with :gblup or :ssgblup with every animal genotyped, which need every genome. A dropped genome cannot be read later (genomes throws), so summaries of past cohorts that need genomes are taken during the run.\n\nDead and culled animals are not candidates, and a record taken at an age the animal did not reach is missing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.Scheme"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BnGsim.SimKey","page":"API Reference","text":"SimKey(seed; replicate = 0, generation = 0, domain = 0, stage = 0, entity = 0, subcounter = 0)\nSimKey(base::SimKey; field = value, ...)\n\nThe coordinate of one random stream:\n\nField Type Meaning\nseed UInt64 master seed of the scenario\nreplicate UInt32 replicate number\ngeneration UInt32 generation or time step\ndomain UInt16 Domain code: meiosis, phenotype, mating, …\nstage UInt16 a stable code of the event within the domain, never a position in a schedule\nentity UInt32 the animal, mating or locus the draws are for; 0 when population-scoped\nsubcounter UInt64 a further index, e.g. sire (0) or dam (1) gamete\n\nThe second form copies base and replaces the named fields, so callers derive keys from a scenario key.\n\nkeyed_rng turns a key into a random number generator. Two keys that differ in any field give unrelated streams.\n\nLayout\n\nThe Philox4x64-10 key is (seed, replicate | KEY_LAYOUT << 32) and the counter is (generation << 32 | domain << 16 | stage, entity, subcounter, 0). The four 64-bit outputs are the state of a Random.Xoshiro. This layout is fixed; the golden test in test/keys.jl guards it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.SimKey"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#BnGsim.SpikeSlab","page":"API Reference","text":"SpikeSlab(π, sd; spike_sd = 0.0)\n\nA spike-and-slab effect distribution: with probability π an effect from the slab Normal(0, sd), otherwise from the spike, exactly 0 (or Normal(0, spike_sd) with spike_sd > 0). One uniform, then one normal when the effect is not exactly zero, per effect. With a point-mass spike, about a fraction 1 - π of the QTL have no effect (scenario family \"spike_slab\"). Only rand, mean (0) and var are defined.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.SpikeSlab"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim._execute-Tuple{Any, Scenario, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"_execute(dir, s, reps; checkpoint_every = 0, should_stop = (r, g) -> false)\n\nRun replicates reps of s with its state in dir/.bngsim-run, or continue a run whose state is there. After step g of replicate r, if should_stop(r, g) the state is saved and (:cancelled, r, g) returned; with checkpoint_every = k > 0 it is also saved every k steps, so that a killed process can be resumed from there. Returns (:completed, summary) with the summaries of all replicates otherwise.\n\nState files: run.jls (scenario JSON, replicates, options, the sessions of the run), history.jls (the shared historical population, once saved), done.jls (the summaries of finished replicates) and current.jls (the replicate in progress: its population, map, loci and the rows so far). on_replicate(r, rep) is called when replicate r has finished, with its population, map, loci and species (e.g. to write its archive).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim._execute"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim.aggregate-Tuple{DataFrames.DataFrame}","page":"API Reference","text":"aggregate(summary::DataFrame) -> DataFrame\n\nAggregate the per-step summaries of several replicates (the table of run_scenario, with a column replicate). One row per time step (generation), with replicates, the number of replicates at that step, and for every other column c of the summary c_mean, c_sd (with the n - 1 divisor) and c_se (c_sd / √n) over the replicates, NaN values left out (n then counts the others). Also aggregated are the changes per step within each replicate: dG (of mean, the index of the traits) and dG_<trait> (of mean_<trait>) for the genetic means, dF, the rate of inbreeding (F_t - F_t-1)(1 - F_t-1), and dFhet, the same rate of the loss of heterozygosity Fhet; and the depletion of the genetic variance within each replicate, var_ratio and genic_ratio, the variance and the genic variance relative to the replicate's first step (with selection, genic_ratio falls with allele frequencies changing at the QTL, and var_ratio below it with the negative disequilibrium of the Bulmer effect).\n\nThe rows are sorted by replicate before summing, so the result is the same, to the bit, whatever the order of the replicates in summary.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.aggregate"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BnGsim.alive","page":"API Reference","text":"alive(pop::Population, t = current_step(pop)) -> Vector{Int}\n\nIds of the animals born at or before time step t that are neither dead nor culled at t.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.alive"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim.cli-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:AbstractString}}","page":"API Reference","text":"cli(args::Vector{String}; stdout = stdout, stderr = stderr) -> Int\n\nThe bngsim command line: run the command in args and return its exit code (ExitCode). bin/bngsim calls it as exit(BnGsim.cli(ARGS)).\n\nvalidate SCENARIO.json: read and check the scenario. Prints {\"status\": \"valid\", \"scenario_sha256\", ...} or {\"status\": \"invalid\", \"errors\": [...]} (exit code 3).\nestimate SCENARIO.json: the expected peak memory, a conservative bound, the disk written and a rough run time (resource_estimate).\nrun SCENARIO.json --out DIR [--replicates A:B] [--memory-limit SIZE]: run the scenario (replicates A:B, all by default) and write to DIR, which must not exist or be empty: scenario.json (the canonical scenario), summary.csv (the per-step summaries of every replicate) and provenance.json. Prints {\"status\": \"completed\", \"outputs\": ...}. With --memory-limit, a scenario whose memory bound exceeds SIZE is refused before it runs (exit code 5). While it runs, the state lives in DIR/.bngsim-run/; a file DIR/CANCEL stops it after the current time step, with the state saved (exit code 7), and with --checkpoint-every K the state is also saved every K steps, so that a killed process can be resumed. With --archive none|chip|qtl|all, an archive of each replicate (write_archive) is written to DIR/archive/r<N>/ when it finishes, and listed in provenance.json.\nmerge DIR... --out OUT: combine completed runs of the same scenario with disjoint replicates (e.g. split over hosts with --replicates) into OUT: scenario.json, summary.csv (rows ordered by replicate, the bytes of a single run of all of them), aggregate.csv and provenance.json listing the runs merged.\nresume DIR: continue a stopped or killed run from its last saved state (removing DIR/CANCEL). The outputs are those of an uninterrupted run, byte for byte; provenance.json lists every session. The state must come from the same BnGsim and Julia versions (exit code 4 otherwise).\nversion [--json]: the BnGsim version, the CLI interface and scenario schema versions, the Julia and package versions and the hash of the active manifest.\n\nsummary.csv depends only on the scenario, the replicates and the package versions: not on the number of threads or the host. provenance.json records the rest of the run (roadmap invariant I2): the canonical scenario and its SHA-256, the versions and manifest hash, the host, CPU, threads, start and end times, and the SHA-256 of each output.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.cli"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim.continue_population-Tuple{FisherWright.FisherWrightResult, AbstractVector{<:Integer}, Real}","page":"API Reference","text":"continue_population(res::FisherWrightResult, ns, mr; M = 1e8, mut_base = 1e8,\n                    rng = Random.default_rng()) -> FisherWrightResult\n\nContinue a FisherWright population for length(ns) further generations with census size ns[g] in generation g, e.g. a decline from the historical size to a breed-like size before founders are sampled. This is fisher_wright(...; init = res, popsize = ns): the same model (dioecious Wright–Fisher, Poisson crossovers at 1/M per bp, infinite-sites mutation at mr / mut_base per bp per meiosis) with a census size that may change every generation.\n\nThe result does not depend on the number of threads. Mutations that fix during the continuation move to substitutions, and the per-generation records of res are extended. An empty ns returns res.\n\nRealizations for a given rng differ from BnGsim 0.1.0, which ran its own serial loop on FisherWright internals.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.continue_population"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim.crossovers-Tuple{DataFrames.DataFrame, AbstractVector{<:Integer}}","page":"API Reference","text":"crossovers(lms::DataFrame, tbp::AbstractVector{<:Integer}; rng::AbstractRNG = Random.default_rng()) -> (Vector{UInt32}, Vector{Int32})\ncrossovers(sp::Species, vm::VariantMap; rng::AbstractRNG = Random.default_rng()) -> (Vector{UInt32}, Vector{Int32})\n\nSample Poisson-distributed crossover positions along the genome without interference, with independent assortment (p = 0.5) at chromosome boundaries.\n\nReturns (gcvs, mcps):\n\ngcvs::Vector{UInt32}: Leading element is the starting parental homologue (1 or 2), followed by genome-wide crossover base-pair positions.\nmcps::Vector{Int32}: Leading element is the starting parental homologue (1 or 2), followed by right-inclusive locus indices in tbp / vm.pos.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.crossovers"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim.dummy_traits-Tuple{Int64, Int64}","page":"API Reference","text":"dummy_traits(n::Int, nlc::Int; rng::AbstractRNG = Random.default_rng())\n\nGenerate n continuous additive BnGStructs.Trait definitions (1 ≤ n ≤ 5) plus a 4-category threshold litter-size trait (\"lts\"), a positive-definite (n + 1) × (n + 1) correlation matrix R, and a dictionary of index weights and variance ratios.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.dummy_traits"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim.eqtl-Tuple{BnGStructs.Haplotype, Union{BnGStructs.LocusSet, AbstractVector{<:Integer}}, AbstractMatrix{<:Real}, AbstractVector{<:BnGStructs.AbstractTrait}}","page":"API Reference","text":"eqtl(hap::Haplotype, loci, R, ts::AbstractVector{<:AbstractTrait};\n     rng = Random.default_rng(), atol = 1e-8) -> MultiTraitQTL\n\nSample additive QTL effects of several traits at shared loci so that the genetic values of the individuals in hap have mean 0, standard deviations ts[i].σₐ and correlation matrix R, exactly in that sample.\n\nEffects of trait i are drawn from ts[i].da, independently across traits. With mathbfC the sample covariance matrix of the resulting genetic values and boldsymbolSigma = mathbfDmathbfRmathbfD (mathbfD the diagonal of the σₐ) the target, the effect matrix mathbfA is replaced by mathbfAmathbfL_C^-topmathbfL_Sigma^top (mathbfL the Cholesky factors). The transform is upper triangular: the effects of trait 1 are only rescaled and keep the shape of their distribution; those of trait i > 1 are linear combinations of the draws for traits 1…i, so only their first two moments are controlled. Means go to the intercepts.\n\nNeeds at least as many QTL as traits and more individuals than traits. The realised covariance is checked and an ArgumentError is thrown if it misses Σ by more than atol (relative to the largest variance).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.eqtl"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim.eqtl-Tuple{BnGStructs.Haplotype, Union{BnGStructs.LocusSet, AbstractVector{<:Integer}}, BnGStructs.AbstractTrait}","page":"API Reference","text":"eqtl(hap::Haplotype, loci, t::AbstractTrait; rng = Random.default_rng(), atol = 1e-8) -> TraitQTL\n\nSample additive QTL effects of trait t at loci (a LocusSet or locus indices) and scale them so that the genetic values of the individuals in hap, the base population, have standard deviation t.σₐ and mean 0.\n\nEffects are drawn from t.da and multiplied by one factor, so their distribution keeps its shape and sign (a Gamma da stays positive).\nThe mean is set by the intercept of the returned TraitQTL (BnGStructs.TraitQTL(...; intercept)), not by shifting the effects.\nt.σₐ is the standard deviation (with the n − 1 divisor) of the genetic values in hap. It includes the covariances between QTL from linkage disequilibrium in that sample; the genic standard deviation, sqrtsum 2p(1-p)a^2, differs from it. trait_summary reports both.\n\nThe realised values are checked after scaling and an ArgumentError is thrown if they miss the target by more than atol (relative), or if the QTL are monomorphic in hap.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.eqtl"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim.evaluate!-Tuple{Population, Scheme}","page":"API Reference","text":"evaluate!(pop::Population, scheme::Scheme) -> Union{Nothing, MME}\n\nFill pop.ebv for every animal and trait by the scheme's evaluation at the current time step. For :pblup, :gblup and :ssgblup the mixed-model equations over the records available (see observed) are built with Breeding.jl and solved directly: y = 1mu + Za + e with sigma^2_e scaling for one trait; for several traits, records stacked trait-major with an intercept per recorded trait, operatornamevar(a) = G_0 otimes K and R_0 among the records of one animal (missing records handled exactly). The covariance components are pop.G0 and pop.R0, first re-estimated by REML if scheme.reml. The equations are returned for prediction error variances; their animal effects are in the rows of evaluation_view, which with scheme.window > 0 covers the recent cohorts and their ancestors only (the EBV of the other animals is NaN). :phenotype needs every trait recorded at birth in both sexes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.evaluate!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BnGsim.evaluation_view","page":"API Reference","text":"evaluation_view(pop::Population, scheme::Scheme, t = current_step(pop)) -> PedigreeView\n\nThe pedigree on which scheme evaluates at step t: every animal when scheme.window is 0, otherwise the animals born in the last window steps and their ancestors traced for scheme.ancestor_generations generations. The animal effects of the equations returned by evaluate! are in the rows of this view.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.evaluation_view"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim.expand-Tuple{BnGStructs.Haplotype, BnGStructs.VariantMap, BnGStructs.Species, Integer, Integer}","page":"API Reference","text":"expand(\n    hap::Haplotype,\n    vm::VariantMap,\n    sp::Species,\n    n_t::Integer,\n    t::Integer;\n    maf::Real = 0.0,\n    rng::AbstractRNG = Random.default_rng(),\n) -> (Haplotype, VariantMap, DataFrame)\n\nExponentially expand a founder population hap (n_0 = hap.nhp ÷ 2 individuals) to n_t individuals over t discrete generations (n_i = round(Int, n_0 * (n_t / n_0)^(i / t))), updating allele frequencies in the returned VariantMap and filtering loci by maf.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.expand"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim.founder_haplotypes-Tuple{AbstractString, AbstractString, Integer, AbstractVector{<:Integer}}","page":"API Reference","text":"founder_haplotypes(fxy::AbstractString, fmp::AbstractString, nid, cbp;\n                   rng = Random.default_rng()) -> (xy::BitMatrix, lmp::DataFrame)\n\nSample nid founders from a historical population stored by xyBnG (the WCGALP base, arm F1): fxy is an xy file holding a locus-major BitMatrix (8-byte header xy-, then nrow, ncol as Int64, then the bits) and fmp a serialized map DataFrame with genome-wide pos.\n\nFisherWright v0.1.x wrote some loci out of order (a few blocks near the end of the genome). Here rows are sorted by pos and chr is recomputed from the cumulative chromosome ends cbp, so the output follows the same conventions as the FisherWrightResult method: loci segregating among the sampled founders, sorted, frq the sample frequency of allele 1.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.founder_haplotypes"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim.founder_haplotypes-Tuple{FisherWright.FisherWrightResult, Integer}","page":"API Reference","text":"founder_haplotypes(res::FisherWrightResult, nid; rng = Random.default_rng())\n    -> (xy::BitMatrix, lmp::DataFrame)\n\nSample nid individuals without replacement from the final generation of res and return their phased haplotypes at the loci segregating among them. Columns 2i-1 and 2i are the two haplotypes of founder i. Alleles are coded ancestral = 0, derived = 1 (no flipping), and lmp.frq is the derived-allele frequency in the sample. lmp.pos is the genome-wide coordinate; lmp.chr the chromosome.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.founder_haplotypes"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim.founder_population-Tuple{BnGStructs.Haplotype, BnGStructs.TraitQTL, BnGStructs.Trait, SimKey}","page":"API Reference","text":"founder_population(hap::Haplotype, qtl::TraitQTL, trait::Trait, key::SimKey) -> Population\nfounder_population(hap::Haplotype, qtl::MultiTraitQTL, traits, key::SimKey; Re = I) -> Population\n\nCohort 0 of a breeding program: the individuals of hap, unrelated founders, with sexes drawn at random (Domain.MATING), genetic values from qtl and one phenotype per trait (Domain.PHENOTYPE). Each trait's residual standard deviation is fixed from the trait as sigma_e = sigma_asqrt(1 - h^2)h^2, with sigma_a the trait's σₐ, so h^2 is the heritability in the founders; Re is the residual correlation matrix of several traits. key carries the seed and replicate; its other fields are set per draw.\n\nThe covariance components of the evaluations are those of the founders: G_0 = DCD, D the diagonal of the traits' σₐ and C the correlation matrix of the founders' genetic values (exactly the R of a multi-trait eqtl), and R_0 = D_e R_e D_e.\n\nA trait's sex (0 females, 1 males, 2 both) limits its records to one sex; its age, a whole number of time steps, is the age at which a record becomes available to evaluations (0: at birth). Phenotypes are drawn for every animal and trait either way, from the animal's own keyed stream, so these settings change which records are seen, not the values drawn.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.founder_population"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim.founder_summary-Tuple{AbstractMatrix{Bool}, DataFrames.DataFrame}","page":"API Reference","text":"founder_summary(xy, lmp; nchip = 20_000, chip_maf = 0.2, nqtl = 10_000, qtl_maf = 0.0,\n                presample = 3, rare = 0.05, ld = (;), rng = Random.default_rng())\n    -> (scalars::NamedTuple, sfs::DataFrame, ld::DataFrame, chip, qtl)\n\nFounder-level outcomes of plan §4.3 for one replicate of one arm. xy holds the founders' phased haplotypes at the loci segregating among them, lmp their map with sample frequencies (founder_haplotypes).\n\nChip and QTL loci are drawn as in the WCGALP pipeline (xyBnG founder_pop): presample × nchip chip candidates with sample MAF > chip_maf are set aside first, and the QTL are drawn from the loci with MAF > qtl_maf that are left, so the QTL are depleted of common loci when the founders have few of them. The chip is nchip of the candidates and nqtl QTL are kept. (The pipeline makes its final cut after the founder expansion, among the candidates that still meet the MAF condition; here it is made among the founders.) presample = 1 removes only the chip, which is what this function did before 2026-11-11 and does not describe the pipeline. Scalars:\n\nnid, nseg: founders, segregating sites;\nsum2pq: Σ2pq over all segregating sites (expected heterozygosity × sites);\nn_maf02: sites with MAF ≥ 0.2, the pool for the chip;\nchip_r2_adj: mean r² between adjacent chip markers on a chromosome, and chip_gap, their mean spacing (bp);\nqtl_maf_mean, qtl_rare: mean QTL MAF and the fraction with MAF < rare;\nqtl_rare_va: share of Σ2pq over QTL carried by QTL with MAF < rare, i.e. the expected share of V_A from rare QTL under i.i.d. effects;\nqtl_maf02: fraction of QTL with MAF ≥ 0.2;\ng_diag, g_offsd: mean diagonal and SD of off-diagonals of the founder-centred VanRaden G at the chip;\nme: 1 / var(G off-diagonal), the effective number of independent chromosome segments implied by realized relationships (Goddard 2009).\n\nsfs is the folded MAF spectrum in 0.05 bins; ld is ld_decay with keyword arguments ld.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.founder_summary"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim.gamete!-Tuple{AbstractVector, AbstractMatrix, DataFrames.DataFrame, AbstractVector{<:Integer}}","page":"API Reference","text":"gamete!(\n    hap::AbstractVector,\n    prt::AbstractMatrix,\n    lms::DataFrame,\n    tbp::AbstractVector{<:Integer};\n    rng::AbstractRNG = Random.default_rng(),\n) -> Vector{UInt32}\n\nGenerate a recombinant gamete hap in-place from a parent's two haplotypes prt (nlc × 2 view), returning the genome-wide crossover positions gcs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.gamete!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim.gene_drop!-Tuple{AbstractMatrix, AbstractMatrix, AbstractMatrix{<:Integer}, DataFrames.DataFrame, AbstractVector{<:Integer}}","page":"API Reference","text":"gene_drop!(\n    og::Haplotype,\n    pg::Haplotype,\n    pm::AbstractMatrix{<:Integer},\n    sp::Species,\n    vm::VariantMap;\n    rng::AbstractRNG = Random.default_rng(),\n) -> DataFrame\ngene_drop!(\n    og::AbstractMatrix,\n    pg::AbstractMatrix,\n    pm::AbstractMatrix{<:Integer},\n    lms::DataFrame,\n    tbp::AbstractVector{<:Integer};\n    rng::AbstractRNG = Random.default_rng(),\n) -> DataFrame\n\nDrop recombined parental gametes from parental haplotypes pg into offspring haplotypes og according to the nf × 2 parent matrix pm ([sire dam] per offspring).\n\nUses per-offspring Xoshiro streams seeded deterministically from rng, so results are invariant to Threads.nthreads(). Returns a DataFrame of crossover records (generation, parent, ihap, crossovers).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.gene_drop!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim.gene_drop-Tuple{AbstractMatrix, AbstractMatrix, AbstractMatrix{<:Integer}, DataFrames.DataFrame, AbstractVector{<:Integer}}","page":"API Reference","text":"gene_drop(pg, og, pm, lms, tbp; rng)\n\nDeprecated: use gene_drop!(og, pg, pm, lms, tbp; rng), which names the mutation of og and takes the offspring first, as the Haplotype method does.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.gene_drop"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim.generation_summary-Tuple{Population, Integer}","page":"API Reference","text":"generation_summary(pop::Population, g; weights = ones(k), segs = nothing) -> NamedTuple\n\nFor cohort g: its size; mean, variance and genic variance sum 2p(1-p)a^2 at the QTL of the genetic values (one trait), or of the aggregate genotype H = wg with weights as w (several traits, then also mean_<name>, var_<name> per trait and the genetic correlation rg_<a>_<b> of each pair within the cohort); the QTL with a non-zero effect on that genetic value (on H, the effects wa) at which the favourable allele is segregating (qtl_seg), fixed (qtl_fixed) or lost (qtl_lost) in the cohort; mean pedigree inbreeding F and mean pedigree coancestry bartheta_g over all pairs of the cohort, self-coancestry (1 + F_i)2 included (founders unrelated and non-inbred); the loss of heterozygosity Fhet; the number of animals alive at step g (all cohorts); and, given the founder segments segs (founder_segments(pop, mm), which step! passes when the scheme records meioses), the mean realized inbreeding Fseg of the cohort, the proportion of the genome at which an animal's two haplotypes descend from the same founder haplotype (its expectation is the pedigree F).\n\nFgrm is the mean genomic inbreeding of the cohort, G_ii - 1 of VanRaden's (2008) first G = ZZ2sum p_0(1-p_0) with the founders' allele frequencies p_0 as base, over all loci segregating in the founders: Z_ij = M_ij - 2p_0j for the dosage M_ij. As p_0 is estimated from the founders, two distinct founder alleles have mean coancestry -1(2n_0 - 1) on this base, so without selection the expectation of Fgrm is about F - (1 - F)(2n_0 - 1), F the pedigree inbreeding (checked over 200 replicates of examples/minimal.json with random selection, steps 2–6); in cohort 0 it is the founders' own deviation from Hardy–Weinberg proportions. Fgrm measures the realized homozygosity of each animal, Fhet the loss of heterozygosity of the cohort.\n\nFhet is 1 - (1 - bartheta_0)bar H_gbar H_0 over all loci, with bar H the mean of 2p(1-p) from the cohort's own allele frequencies and bartheta_0 = 1(2n_0) the founders' mean coancestry. Each bar H_g is a sample estimate whose expectation is H(1 - bartheta_g), H the heterozygosity of the population the founders were drawn from, so without selection the expectation of Fhet is the mean coancestry bartheta_g. Under selection, Fhet − coancestry measures the extra loss of heterozygosity at loci linked to QTL; both carry drift noise that is large when few chromosome segments segregate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.generation_summary"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BnGsim.genomes","page":"API Reference","text":"genomes(pop::Population[, ids]) -> Haplotype\n\nThe phased genomes of the animals ids (default: all), two haplotypes per animal in the order of ids. An ArgumentError if the genome of one of them was dropped (retain = :active in Scheme); see retained.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.genomes"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BnGsim.genotyped","page":"API Reference","text":"genotyped(pop::Population, scheme::Scheme, t = current_step(pop)) -> Vector{Int}\n\nThe animals genotyped for :gblup (all) and :ssgblup (those born in the last scheme.genotyped_cohorts cohorts at step t, or all when it is 0).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.genotyped"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim.historical_population-Tuple{HistoricalSpec}","page":"API Reference","text":"historical_population(spec::HistoricalSpec; seed = nothing, rng = Xoshiro(seed),\n                      verbose = false, progress_interval = 1000) -> FisherWrightResult\n\nRun the historical population described by spec. Give either an integer seed, which starts a fresh Xoshiro(seed) stream, or an rng. The global RNG is not touched. FisherWright draws every haplotype from its own stream seeded from rng, so the result does not depend on the number of threads.\n\nA given seed gives the same population as in BnGsim 0.1.0, where the global RNG was seeded with it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.historical_population"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim.id_to_row-Tuple{PedigreeView, Integer}","page":"API Reference","text":"id_to_row(v::PedigreeView, id) -> Int\n\nThe row of animal id in the view; an ArgumentError if it is not in it (elementwise for a vector of ids).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.id_to_row"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim.index_weights-Tuple{Population, Scheme}","page":"API Reference","text":"index_weights(pop::Population, scheme::Scheme) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nThe index weights of scheme for the traits of pop (all 1 when the scheme gives none).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.index_weights"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim.infinitesimal-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Integer}, AbstractVector{<:Integer}, AbstractMatrix, AbstractMatrix, SimKey}","page":"API Reference","text":"infinitesimal(sire, dam, G0, R0, key::SimKey; mean = zeros(size(G0, 1)))\n    -> (u::Matrix{Float64}, y::Matrix{Float64})\n\nBreeding values and phenotypes of k = size(G0, 1) traits under the infinitesimal model, exactly u sim N(0 G_0 otimes A), for the animals of a pedigree (sire, dam: 0 for unknown; parents before offspring). Each animal gets u_i = tfrac12 (u_s + u_d) + m_i with the Mendelian sampling term m_i sim N(0 d_i G_0), d_i = 1 - tfrac14 (1 + F_s) - tfrac14 (1 + F_d) (a term only for each known parent; F the inbreeding coefficients), and y_i = mu + u_i + e_i with e_i sim N(0 R_0). u and y are n × k.\n\nThe draws of animal i come from SimKey(key; domain = Domain.INFINITESIMAL, entity = i), so the values of an animal do not depend on the others or on the order of computation. These are data whose genetic model is the animal model itself (an \"Architecture A\" for benchmarks), unlike the finite-locus genetic values of the breeding loop.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.infinitesimal"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim.keyed_int-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Random.AbstractRNG, T, T}} where T<:Integer","page":"API Reference","text":"keyed_int(rng, a, b)\n\nA uniform integer in a:b drawn from raw 64-bit outputs of rng, by an algorithm that does not change with the Julia version.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.keyed_int"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim.keyed_rng-Tuple{SimKey}","page":"API Reference","text":"keyed_rng(k::SimKey) -> Random.Xoshiro\n\nThe random number generator of coordinate k: a Xoshiro whose state is the Philox4x64-10 image of the coordinate. It is a pure function of k; use it for all the draws of that coordinate and for nothing else.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.keyed_rng"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim.keyed_shuffle!-Tuple{Random.AbstractRNG, AbstractVector}","page":"API Reference","text":"keyed_shuffle!(rng, v)\n\nFisher–Yates shuffle of v with keyed_int; the same rng state gives the same order on every Julia version.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.keyed_shuffle!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim.ld_decay-Tuple{AbstractMatrix{Bool}, DataFrames.DataFrame}","page":"API Reference","text":"ld_decay(xy, lmp; minmaf = 0.05, edges = [5e3, 1e4, 2e4, 5e4, 1e5, 2e5, 5e5, 1e6, 2e6, 5e6],\n         npairs = 20_000, M = 1e8, rng = Random.default_rng()) -> DataFrame\n\nHaplotype r² between loci with sample MAF ≥ minmaf, in distance bins [edges[k], edges[k+1]) bp. For each bin, npairs pairs on the same chromosome are drawn by picking a random anchor locus and a random partner in the bin downstream of it.\n\nColumns: lo, hi, d (mean distance of the sampled pairs), n, r2 (mean), r2adj = r2 - 1/nh (sample-size correction for nh phased haplotypes), and the Sved (1971) LD-based effective size ne = (1/r2adj - 1) / (4c), c = d/M Morgan, which refers to about t = 1/(2c) generations ago (Hayes et al. 2003). ne is NaN when r2adj ≤ 0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.ld_decay"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim.load_checkpoint-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API Reference","text":"load_checkpoint(path; strict = true) -> Population\n\nRestore a population saved by save_checkpoint. With strict, a checkpoint written by another version of BnGsim or Julia is refused, since the serialization format may differ; strict = false tries anyway.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.load_checkpoint"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim.make_progeny!-Tuple{BnGStructs.Haplotype, BnGStructs.Haplotype, AbstractMatrix{<:Integer}, MeiosisMap, SimKey}","page":"API Reference","text":"make_progeny!(offspring::Haplotype, parents::Haplotype, plan, mm::MeiosisMap, key::SimKey;\n              record = false) -> offspring, or (offspring, MeiosisRecord)\n\nMake one offspring per row of the mating plan plan (n × 2, sire and dam as columns of parents): row i writes haplotypes 2i-1 (from the sire) and 2i (from the dam) of offspring, which must have at least 2n haplotypes and the loci of parents.\n\nThe draws of gamete (i, k) come from keyed_rng(SimKey(key; domain = Domain.MEIOSIS, entity = i, subcounter = k - 1)), k = 1 for the sire and 2 for the dam. The result is a function of key, the plan and the parents only: it does not depend on the number of threads or on the order in which rows are processed. Several offspring of one pair are separate rows. The caller sets seed, replicate, generation and stage of key; its domain, entity and subcounter are overwritten.\n\nSee MeiosisMap for the meiosis model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.make_progeny!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim.mating_plan-Tuple{Population, Vararg{Any, 4}}","page":"API Reference","text":"mating_plan(pop, sires, nsire_off, dams, ndam_off) -> Matrix{Int}\n\nPair sire and dam slots at random (a keyed shuffle of the dam slots, Domain.MATING): row i is mating i, as ids.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.mating_plan"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim.meioses-Tuple{Population}","page":"API Reference","text":"meioses(pop::Population) -> RelationshipMatrices.Meioses\n\nThe crossover records of all cohorts in the form of RelationshipMatrices.Meioses: gamete 2k − 1 of the k-th animal of a cohort is its haplotype from the sire, 2k from the dam. Every cohort must have been recorded (Scheme(; record_meiosis = true) from the first step on).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.meioses"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim.norm_mv!-Tuple{AbstractMatrix, AbstractVector{Float64}}","page":"API Reference","text":"norm_mv!(Q::AbstractMatrix, v::AbstractVector{Float64}; ϵ::Real = 1e-6, μ::Real = 0.0, σ::Real = 1.0) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nDeprecated: eqtl no longer uses it, because shifting every effect to centre the genetic values changes the shape of the effect distribution. Use the intercept of a TraitQTL instead.\n\nNormalize additive QTL effect vector v in-place such that the genetic values Q' * v (where Q is the nqtl × nid dosage matrix) have sample mean μ and standard deviation σ within tolerance ϵ. Returns the normalized breeding values Q' * v.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.norm_mv!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BnGsim.observed","page":"API Reference","text":"observed(pop::Population, t = current_step(pop)) -> BitMatrix\n\nThe n × k pattern of the records available to an evaluation at time step t: entry (i, j) is true if animal i expresses trait j (by sex), is at least the trait's age old, and was alive at that age (an animal that died or was culled earlier has no record).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.observed"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim.ocs_contributions-Tuple{AbstractVector, AbstractMatrix, AbstractVector, Real, AbstractVector{Bool}}","page":"API Reference","text":"ocs_contributions(u, Θ, b, K, male) -> (c, feasible, value)\n\nContributions c (each sex summing to ½, all ge 0) maximising uc subject to f(c) = cTheta c + 2bc le K: optimum contribution selection (Meuwissen 1997) with the linear term b that the fixed contributions of older age classes add.\n\nThe problem is convex. It is solved exactly by homotopy in the weight nu of the gain: c(nu) minimises f(c) - 2nu uc under the sex constraints and c ge 0. For a fixed set of zero contributions c(nu) = y + nu x is linear. The path starts at nu = infty, the best male and the best female (ties by position), and nu is lowered from one breakpoint to the next (a candidate entering when its multiplier reaches zero, or a contribution reaching zero) until f(c(nu)) = K. The free set stays as small as the solution, so a breakpoint costs a factorisation of the order of the number of parents. If the path reaches nu = 0 first, c(0) minimises f, the bound cannot be met, and it is returned with feasible = false. value is f(c) at the solution.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.ocs_contributions"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim.pack_loci-Tuple{AbstractMatrix{Bool}, AbstractVector{<:Integer}}","page":"API Reference","text":"pack_loci(xy::AbstractMatrix{Bool}, loci) -> Matrix{UInt64}\n\nPack the haplotypes at loci (rows of xy) into 64-bit words, one column per locus, so that allele and haplotype counts between two loci are popcounts.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.pack_loci"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim.parse_scenario-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API Reference","text":"parse_scenario(json::AbstractString) -> Scenario\nparse_scenario(d::AbstractDict) -> Scenario\n\nRead a scenario from JSON text (or a parsed object) and validate it. Throws a ScenarioError that lists every problem found.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.parse_scenario"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BnGsim.pedigree","page":"API Reference","text":"pedigree(pop::Population[, ids]) -> DataFrame\n\nThe animals ids of pop (default: all), one row each, with the id explicit, so that a subset, a reordering or a concatenation of tables keeps the identity of every animal:\n\nid, sire, dam (0 = unknown; parents are ids, and may be absent from a subset), male, generation (the birth cohort), exit (the first time step at which the animal is dead or culled; missing while it lives);\nper trait <name>: tbv_<name>, the true genetic value; phenotype_<name>, the phenotype (missing in the sex that does not express the trait; it is drawn at birth, and is available to an evaluation only from the trait's age on, see observed); and ebv_<name>, the last evaluation (missing before one, and for animals outside an evaluation window, see Scheme).\n\nInside pop, ids equal rows; use the id column, not the row number, once a table leaves it. PedigreeView builds the dense rows of relationship matrices from these ids.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.pedigree"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim.pedigree_view-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Integer}, AbstractVector{<:Integer}, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"pedigree_view(sire, dam, ids; generations = typemax(Int)) -> PedigreeView\npedigree_view(pop::Population, ids; generations = typemax(Int)) -> PedigreeView\n\nThe view of the animals ids and their ancestors, traced through the pedigree for up to generations generations (parents are generation 1, grandparents 2; 0 gives ids alone). With all generations traced, the relationships among ids computed on the view equal those of the full pedigree; parents beyond the traced depth become unknown, and their offspring are treated as base animals.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.pedigree_view"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim.phenotype!-Tuple{DataFrames.DataFrame, BnGStructs.AbstractTrait}","page":"API Reference","text":"phenotype!(\n    ped::DataFrame,\n    trts::Union{AbstractTrait, AbstractVector{<:AbstractTrait}};\n    R_e::Union{Nothing, AbstractMatrix{<:Real}} = nothing,\n    rng::AbstractRNG = Random.default_rng(),\n) -> DataFrame\n\nGenerate phenotypes in ped (columns \"ft_\" * t.name) from true breeding values (\"tbv_\" * t.name) for one or more BnGStructs.AbstractTrait objects (aTrait or tTrait).\n\nEnvironmental standard deviation for trait t is sigma_e = sigma_a sqrt(1 - h^2)  h^2.\nIf R_e (a residual correlation matrix across trts) is given, environmental deviates are drawn jointly with correlation R_e.\nSex-limited traits (t.sex ∈ (0, 1)) set records for the non-expressing sex in ped.sex to missing.\nThreshold traits (tTrait) discretize the latent liability L = textTBV + e against t.threshold into categories 0, 1, ..., length(t.threshold).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.phenotype!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim.philox4x64-Tuple{Tuple{UInt64, UInt64}, NTuple{4, UInt64}}","page":"API Reference","text":"philox4x64(key::NTuple{2,UInt64}, ctr::NTuple{4,UInt64}) -> NTuple{4,UInt64}\n\nThe Philox4x64-10 block function: a keyed bijection of 256-bit counters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.philox4x64"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BnGsim.provenance","page":"API Reference","text":"provenance(io::IO = stdout; seed = nothing, extra = Dict{String,Any}())\n\nWrite a run header recording what is needed to reproduce a simulation: the date, host, CPU, Julia version, thread count, BLAS threads, the seed, and for each JuliaBnG package its version, git commit and whether its working tree was dirty. Lines are prefixed with # so the header can sit on top of a plain-text log or table.\n\nResults from FisherWright.fisher_wright are reproducible for a given seed only at a fixed thread count, which is why the thread count is recorded.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.provenance"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim.r2-Tuple{Matrix{UInt64}, Integer, Integer, Integer}","page":"API Reference","text":"r2(W, i, j, nh) -> Float64\n\nSquared correlation of alleles between packed loci i and j over nh phased haplotypes (Hill and Robertson 1968). NaN if either is monomorphic.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.r2"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim.random_mate-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{T, T}} where T<:Integer","page":"API Reference","text":"random_mate(n1::Integer, n2::Integer; rng::AbstractRNG = Random.default_rng()) -> (Matrix{Int}, Vector{Bool})\nrandom_mate(sex::AbstractVector{Bool}, n2::Integer; rng::AbstractRNG = Random.default_rng()) -> Matrix{Int}\n\nRandomly draw sire and dam pairs from n1 parents (or parents with sexes sex) to produce n2 offspring.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.random_mate"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim.read_packed_haplotypes-Tuple{AbstractString, AbstractVector{<:Integer}}","page":"API Reference","text":"read_packed_haplotypes(path, cbp; rng = Random.default_rng())\n    -> (xy::BitMatrix, lmp::DataFrame)\n\nRead phased haplotypes written by an external simulator (e.g. the msprime driver of the founder-design paper) in the BNGH1 format: 8-byte magic \"BNGH1\\0\\0\\0\", Int64 nloci and nhap, UInt32 genome-wide positions, then each locus as cld(nhap, 8) bytes with haplotype h in bit (h-1) % 8 of byte (h-1) ÷ 8 (numpy packbits(bitorder = \"little\")). 1 is the derived allele; haplotypes 2i-1, 2i form individual i.\n\nReturns the same layout as the FisherWrightResult method: loci segregating in the sample, sorted by position, chr from the cumulative chromosome ends cbp, frq the sample frequency of allele 1, and random ref/alt bases.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.read_packed_haplotypes"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim.read_scenario-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API Reference","text":"read_scenario(path) -> Scenario\n\nRead and validate the scenario in the JSON file path.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.read_scenario"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BnGsim.recorded","page":"API Reference","text":"recorded(pop::Population, t = current_step(pop); trait = 1) -> Vector{Int}\n\nIds of the animals whose phenotype of trait number trait is available to an evaluation at time step t, i.e. expressed in their sex and of age at least the trait's age. See also observed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.recorded"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim.resource_estimate-Tuple{Scenario}","page":"API Reference","text":"resource_estimate(s::Scenario) -> NamedTuple\n\nThe resources scenario s needs, from the scenario alone:\n\npeak_rss_bytes: the expected peak resident memory of a run of one replicate at a time, the sum of memory_terms: the process itself, the historical population, the packed genomes of all animals (and with them the pedigree and records), and the dense relationship matrices of :gblup and :ssgblup;\npeak_rss_bound_bytes: a conservative bound for admission: the expectation times a factor that no calibration or held-out run exceeded, with a margin;\ndisk_bytes: the files bngsim run writes;\nseconds: a rough run time of all replicates (historical population and breeding loop), on the calibration host within a factor of about two;\nsize: the ScenarioSize the terms are computed from.\n\nThe coefficients are calibrated on one host and thread count (see the documentation page \"Resource estimate\"); memory and time differ on others, and the garbage collector makes the peak of a given run vary by about 10 %. Replicates run one after the other, so memory does not grow with their number.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.resource_estimate"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim.retained-Tuple{Population}","page":"API Reference","text":"retained(pop::Population) -> Vector{Int}\n\nThe ids of the animals whose genomes are kept.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.retained"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim.rnd_ped-Tuple{Integer, Integer}","page":"API Reference","text":"rnd_ped(nn::Integer, ng::Integer; rng::AbstractRNG = Random.default_rng()) -> DataFrame\n\nGenerate a random-mating discrete-generation pedigree with nn individuals per generation across a founder cohort (grt = 0) and ng descendant generations (1:ng).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.rnd_ped"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim.row_to_id-Tuple{PedigreeView, Integer}","page":"API Reference","text":"row_to_id(v::PedigreeView, r) -> Int\n\nThe id of row r of the view (elementwise for a vector of rows).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.row_to_id"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#BnGsim.run_replicate","page":"API Reference","text":"run_replicate(s::Scenario, r = 1; history = nothing)\n    -> (; population, summary, species, map, qtl, chip)\n\nRun replicate r of scenario s: the historical population (or history, a FisherWrightResult to start from), the founders, the QTL and chip loci, the traits, then simulate! for s.steps steps with the Scheme of the scenario. Every random draw comes from a key of SimKey(s.seed; replicate = r) (the historical population from replicate 0 when it is shared), so a replicate does not depend on the others or on the number of threads.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.run_replicate"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim.run_scenario-Tuple{Scenario}","page":"API Reference","text":"run_scenario(s::Scenario; replicates = 1:s.replicates) -> DataFrame\nrun_scenario(path::AbstractString; kw...) -> DataFrame\n\nRun the replicates of s and return their per-step summaries (simulate!) in one table, with a first column replicate. A shared historical population is simulated once.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.run_scenario"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim.sample_founder_population-Tuple{BnGStructs.Species, BnGStructs.Haplotype, Union{BnGStructs.VariantMap, DataFrames.DataFrame}}","page":"API Reference","text":"sample_founder_population(\n    species::Species,\n    hap::Haplotype,\n    vm_or_df::Union{VariantMap, DataFrame};\n    n_id::Int = hap.nhp ÷ 2,\n    n_marker::Int = 1000,\n    n_qtl::Int = 100,\n    h2::Float64 = 0.5,\n    trait_name::String = \"trait1\",\n    qtl_dist::Distribution = Normal(0.0, 1.0),\n    rng::AbstractRNG = Random.default_rng(),\n) -> FounderPopulation\n\nConstruct a founder population directly from an existing pre-computed Haplotype and VariantMap (or map DataFrame).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.sample_founder_population"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim.sample_founder_population-Tuple{BnGStructs.Species}","page":"API Reference","text":"sample_founder_population(\n    species::Species;\n    ne::Integer,\n    n_id::Int = 100,\n    n_marker::Int = 1000,\n    n_qtl::Int = 100,\n    h2::Float64 = 0.5,\n    trait_name::String = \"trait1\",\n    qtl_dist::Distribution = Normal(0.0, 1.0),\n    burnin_generations::Int = 500,\n    mutation_rate::Float64 = 1.0,\n    rng::AbstractRNG = Random.default_rng(),\n    verbose::Bool = false,\n) -> FounderPopulation\n\nSimulate a base founder population with n_marker markers, n_qtl QTLs, and heritability h2 by running a Fisher-Wright burn-in of burnin_generations generations of ne diploid individuals on the genome of species (a species carries no population size since BnGStructs 0.4, so ne is required).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.sample_founder_population"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim.sample_id-Tuple{BnGStructs.Haplotype, BnGStructs.VariantMap, Integer}","page":"API Reference","text":"sample_id(\n    hap::Haplotype,\n    vm::VariantMap,\n    ne::Integer;\n    maf::Real = 0.0,\n    rng::AbstractRNG = Random.default_rng(),\n) -> (Haplotype, VariantMap)\n\nSample ne diploid individuals without replacement from hap, recompute sample allele frequencies in the returned VariantMap, and retain loci satisfying maf < frq < 1 - maf.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.sample_id"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim.sample_loci-Tuple{BnGStructs.Haplotype, BnGStructs.VariantMap, Vararg{BnGStructs.SNPSet}}","page":"API Reference","text":"sample_loci(\n    hap::Haplotype,\n    vm::VariantMap,\n    sets::SNPSet...;\n    rng::AbstractRNG = Random.default_rng(),\n) -> Vector{LocusSet}\n\nSample one or more BnGStructs.LocusSet panels from vm according to the specifications in sets::SNPSet... (locus count nlc, maf threshold, and exclusive flag).\n\nAllele frequencies are taken from vm.frq if non-empty, or computed directly from hap.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.sample_loci"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim.save_checkpoint-Tuple{AbstractString, Population}","page":"API Reference","text":"save_checkpoint(path, pop::Population) -> path\n\nSave the whole state of pop at a time-step boundary. A run continued from load_checkpoint with the same scheme and MeiosisMap is identical to an uninterrupted one, because every draw is keyed by coordinate and nothing else carries state between steps.\n\nThe file is a magic, the versions of BnGsim and Julia, and the population in Julia's Serialization format. That format is not guaranteed across Julia or package versions, so a checkpoint is for resuming a run, not for archiving results; write haplotypes with BnGStructs.write_haplotypes for that.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.save_checkpoint"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim.scenario_dict-Tuple{Scenario}","page":"API Reference","text":"scenario_dict(s::Scenario) -> OrderedDict-like JSON object\n\nThe canonical JSON object of s: every key, with defaults filled.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.scenario_dict"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim.scenario_json-Tuple{Scenario}","page":"API Reference","text":"scenario_json(s::Scenario) -> String\n\nThe canonical JSON text of s (two-space indentation, every key present). Reading it back gives a scenario equal to s, and writing that gives the same text.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.scenario_json"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim.select_parents-Tuple{Population, Scheme, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"select_parents(pop::Population, scheme::Scheme, m) -> (sires, nsire_off, dams, ndam_off, info)\n\nParents and their numbers of offspring (each list summing to scheme.noff), among the living males of age at most scheme.max_sire_age and females of age at most scheme.max_dam_age at the current time step (the youngest cohort only, with the default ages 0), ranked on the index what a (see Scheme). m is the MME returned by evaluate! (nothing for :tbv and :phenotype); :rocs takes the prediction error covariance of the candidates' index from it. Every ranking breaks ties by ascending id (invariant I1a). info is empty except for :ocs, where it holds the bound, the constrained kinship reached, the generation interval used and whether the bound could be met (ocs_bound, ocs_kinship, ocs_L, ocs_feasible). With ocs = :fixed the bound is K/2 and the kinship cAc2 of Breeding.TM1997's contributions, on the same kinship scale, and ocs_L is NaN; when K cannot be met, TM1997 returns the minimum-coancestry contributions and ocs_feasible is false.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.select_parents"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim.simulate!-Tuple{Population, Scheme, MeiosisMap, Integer}","page":"API Reference","text":"simulate!(pop::Population, scheme::Scheme, mm::MeiosisMap, nstep; checkpoint = nothing) -> DataFrame\n\nRun nstep time steps of scheme with step!; one row per cohort in which selection took place, plus the summary of the last cohort (without accuracy, parents and generation interval). With checkpoint a path, the population is saved there with save_checkpoint after every step (atomically: written to a temporary file, then renamed).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.simulate!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim.step!-Tuple{Population, Scheme, MeiosisMap}","page":"API Reference","text":"step!(pop::Population, scheme::Scheme, mm::MeiosisMap) -> NamedTuple\n\nOne time step: evaluate, select, mate, and add the offspring (with genetic values, sexes and phenotypes) as the next cohort. Returns the summary of the current cohort (see generation_summary) with the accuracy of the evaluation in it (of the index, and with several traits of each trait as accuracy_<name>), the parents used, the generation interval L (mean age of the parents at the birth of their offspring, over both sexes), the realized selection intensities intensity_sire and intensity_dam (the mean index of a sex's parents, weighted by their numbers of offspring, less the mean index of the eligible candidates of that sex, in standard deviations of the candidates' index), the mean reliability R^2 = 1 - mathrmPEVsigma^2_a of the candidates' index (reliability, and reliability_<name> per trait with several traits; sigma^2_a of the index wG_0w from the base variances the evaluation used, NaN unless the scheme sets report_reliability and the evaluation solves mixed-model equations) and the variances used by the evaluation, va and ve (one trait) or va_<name> and ve_<name>.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.step!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim.sum_map-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Integer}}","page":"API Reference","text":"sum_map(sp::Species) -> DataFrame\nsum_map(chr_len::AbstractVector{<:Integer}; M::Real = 1e8) -> DataFrame\nsum_map(vm::VariantMap; cM::Real = 1e6) -> DataFrame\n\nConstruct a chromosome linkage map summary DataFrame.\n\nFor chr_len or sp::Species, returns columns chr::Int8, len::Float64 (length in Morgans), and ebp::UInt32 (cumulative ending base-pair coordinate).\nFor vm::VariantMap, summarizes per-chromosome span in Morgans (len), locus count (nlc::UInt32), and 1-based starting locus index (bgn::UInt32).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.sum_map"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim.tbv!-Tuple{DataFrames.DataFrame, Union{BnGStructs.Genotype, BnGStructs.Haplotype}, BnGStructs.TraitQTL}","page":"API Reference","text":"tbv!(ped::DataFrame, hap::Union{Haplotype, Genotype}, qtl::TraitQTL) -> DataFrame\ntbv!(ped::DataFrame, hap::Union{Haplotype, Genotype}, mqtl::MultiTraitQTL) -> DataFrame\n\nCompute True Breeding Values (TBV) using BnGStructs.tbv and store them in ped as column(s) \"tbv_\" * trait_name.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.tbv!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim.trait_summary-Tuple{BnGStructs.Haplotype, Union{BnGStructs.MultiTraitQTL, BnGStructs.TraitQTL}}","page":"API Reference","text":"trait_summary(hap::Haplotype, qtl::Union{TraitQTL, MultiTraitQTL}) -> NamedTuple\n\nGenetic summary of the individuals in hap for the traits of qtl (additive architecture), one entry per trait:\n\nmean, var: mean and variance (n − 1 divisor) of the genetic values;\ngenic: genic variance sum_j 2p_j(1-p_j)a_j^2, with p_j the allele frequency at QTL j in hap; var − genic is the contribution of linkage disequilibrium (and of the n − 1 divisor);\ncor: correlation matrix of the genetic values (1 × 1 for one trait).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.trait_summary"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim.validate_scenario-Tuple{Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"validate_scenario(json) -> Vector{String}\n\nThe problems of a scenario (JSON text or a parsed object), each prefixed by its path; empty if it is valid.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.validate_scenario"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim.vanraden_g-Tuple{AbstractMatrix{Bool}, AbstractVector{<:Integer}}","page":"API Reference","text":"vanraden_g(xy, loci) -> Matrix{Float64}\n\nVanRaden (2008) method-1 genomic relationships among the individuals of xy (columns 2i-1, 2i) at loci, centred on the allele frequencies of the same individuals: G = ZZ' / 2Σpq. Computed by RelationshipMatrices.grm.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.vanraden_g"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim.write_archive-Tuple{AbstractString, Population, BnGStructs.VariantMap, BnGStructs.Species}","page":"API Reference","text":"write_archive(dir, pop::Population, vm::VariantMap, sp::Species;\n              panel = Int32[], qtl = Int32[], t = current_step(pop)) -> Vector{String}\n\nWrite the population pop to directory dir (created if needed) and return the files written:\n\npedigree.csv: id, sire, dam, sex, generation, exit (0 for an unknown parent; sex M or F; generation the birth cohort; exit the first time step at which the animal was dead or culled, empty while it lives);\nrecords.csv: id and one column per trait, the phenotype where it was recorded by time step t (expressed in the animal's sex, at or after the trait's age, alive at that age; observed), empty otherwise;\ntruth.csv: id, then tbv_<trait> (the true genetic value) and ebv_<trait> (the last evaluation, empty if none) for each trait;\ntraits.csv: one row per trait: name, h2, sigma_a, mu, sex, age, intercept (the constant of its genetic value);\nqtl.csv (if qtl is given, the locus indices of the QTL in vm): locus, chr, bp, then the additive effect of one copy of the allele coded 1 for each trait;\nwith panel (locus indices in vm, e.g. the chip), the genotypes of every animal whose genome is kept at those loci, in PLINK 1 binary format: genotypes.bed (SNP-major), genotypes.bim (chr, the id l<locus>, the position in centimorgans within the chromosome from the map or, without one, from the species' genetic length, the position in bp within the chromosome, the allele coded 1 as A1 and the other as A2) and genotypes.fam (family 1, the animal id, sire, dam, sex 1/2, phenotype -9).\n\nNumbers are written in Julia's shortest round-trip form. Animals keep the ids of the population. In the .bed file the allele coded 1 is A1, the fifth column of the .bim: PLINK 1.9 counts it with --recode A --keep-allele-order (without that flag it makes A1 the minor allele of the sample), plink2 with --export A --export-allele on that column (by default it counts the sixth). Both were checked to read back the genotypes exactly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.write_archive"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim.write_scenario-Tuple{AbstractString, Scenario}","page":"API Reference","text":"write_scenario(path, s::Scenario)\n\nWrite the canonical JSON of s to path.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.write_scenario"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim.write_xy-Tuple{AbstractString, BitMatrix}","page":"API Reference","text":"write_xy(path, xy::BitMatrix)\n\nWrite a locus-major BitMatrix in xyBnG's xy format (8-byte header xy- with zero type bytes, Int64 nrow and ncol, then the bits), the format read by xyBnG's pipeline and by founder_haplotypes(fxy, fmp, …).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.write_xy"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#RelationshipMatrices.founder_segments-Tuple{Population, MeiosisMap}","page":"API Reference","text":"founder_segments(pop::Population, mm::MeiosisMap) -> RelationshipMatrices.HaplotypeSegments\n\nThe founder haplotype of every base of every animal, reconstructed from the recorded meioses (see meioses) and the pedigree, on the chromosomes of mm. Founder haplotype h is haplotype h of cohort 0. RelationshipMatrices.irm(segs) gives the realized IBD relationship matrix and segment_inbreeding(segs) the realized inbreeding, without sampling loci.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"RelationshipMatrices.founder_segments"},{"category":"module","location":"api/#BnGsim.Domain","page":"API Reference","text":"Domain\n\nCodes of the domain field of a SimKey: which part of a simulation a stream belongs to. The values are part of the stream layout and never change; new domains get new codes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.Domain"},{"category":"module","location":"api/#BnGsim.ExitCode","page":"API Reference","text":"ExitCode\n\nThe exit codes of the bngsim command line. They are part of its interface (cli_interface 1) and do not change meaning.\n\nCode Name Meaning\n0 OK success\n2 USAGE unknown command, missing or invalid arguments\n3 INVALID_SCENARIO the scenario file is missing, unreadable or does not validate\n4 REJECTED refused before running: the output directory exists and is not empty\n5 BUDGET_EXCEEDED refused before running: the memory bound of resource_estimate exceeds --memory-limit\n6 SIMULATION_FAILED the simulation stopped with an explicit error (e.g. too few loci pass a maf filter)\n7 CANCELLED stopped at a step boundary by a file DIR/CANCEL (which bin/bngsim creates on SIGINT or SIGTERM); bngsim resume DIR continues\n70 INTERNAL_ERROR an unexpected error (a bug)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.ExitCode"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim.FisherWrightAdapter.census-Tuple{FisherWright.FisherWrightResult}","page":"API Reference","text":"census(res::FisherWrightResult) -> Vector{Int}\n\nThe census sizes of the recorded generations, N₀, N₁, …: res.records.census from FisherWright 0.10, res.census before.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.FisherWrightAdapter.census"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#BnGsim.FisherWrightAdapter.dense_haplotypes-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"dense_haplotypes(muts, ends; rng) -> (BitMatrix, DataFrame)\n\nmuts2bitarray(muts, ends): the loci segregating among the haplotypes muts (rows, genome-wide positions) by haplotype (columns), and the map. The placeholder nucleotides of the map come from rng where FisherWright accepts one (0.10 and later), from the global generator before.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BnGsim.FisherWrightAdapter.dense_haplotypes"},{"category":"section","location":"roadmap/#Roadmap","page":"Roadmap","text":"This page is the single development plan for BnGsim.jl. It supersedes the working drafts plan.md, plan-gemini.md, plan-claude.md and plan-unified.md (August 2026). It also takes in the November 2026 reorganization of the JuliaBnG ecosystem, which moved forward simulation out of Breeding.jl into BnGsim and left genetic evaluation and optimum-contribution selection in Breeding.jl.\n\nUpstream names cited below were checked against BnGStructs 0.3.0, FisherWright 0.8.0, RelationshipMatrices 0.7.0 and Breeding 0.3.0. If the installed packages disagree with this page, the installed packages win and this page is amended.","title":"Roadmap"},{"category":"section","location":"roadmap/#1.-Purpose-and-package-boundaries","page":"Roadmap","text":"BnGsim.jl simulates breeding programs. It models founder populations, meiosis, traits and records, lifecycle events, selection and mating, and reproducible execution. It does not solve mixed-model equations, build relationship matrices, or run forward sequence-level burn-in. It calls the sibling packages for those.\n\n                  ┌──────────────────────────────────────┐\n                  │             BnGStructs               │\n                  │ (Species, Haplotype, VariantMap,     │\n                  │  LocusSet, Trait, TraitQTL, tbv)     │\n                  └──────┬───────────┬────────────┬──────┘\n                         │           │            │\n         ┌───────────────┘           │            └───────────────┐\n         ▼                           ▼                            ▼\n┌─────────────────┐      ┌────────────────────────┐     ┌──────────────────┐\n│  FisherWright   │      │  RelationshipMatrices  │     │   Breeding.jl    │\n│ (historical WF  │      │ (A, A⁻¹, G, H⁻¹, IRM,  │────►│ (MME, solvers,   │\n│  burn-in, muts) │      │  DRM, APY, kinship)    │     │  REML/Gibbs,     │\n└────────┬────────┘      └───────────┬────────────┘     │  GBLUP/Bayes,    │\n         │                           │                  │  OCS/ROCS)       │\n         └───────────────┬───────────┴──────────────────┴─────────┬────────┘\n                         ▼                                        │\n                  ┌───────────────────────────────────────────────▼──┐\n                  │                     BnGsim                       │\n                  │ (founder sampling, meiosis/gene-drop, QTL and    │\n                  │  phenotype simulation, selection/mating loops,   │\n                  │  historical/founder summaries, provenance)       │\n                  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n\nPackage Responsibility as seen from BnGsim\nFisherWright.jl The only founder-population engine. Diploid, dioecious Wright–Fisher with infinite-sites mutation, recombination maps, demography (bottleneck, growth) and optional fitness or trait selection. BnGsim calls fisher_wright and to_haplotype; it does not use quickGT or quickHap.\nBnGStructs.jl The standard data types: packed phased Haplotype, Genotype, VariantMap, LocusSet/SNPSet, Species, trait definitions (aTrait, tTrait), TraitQTL/MultiTraitQTL and tbv.\nRelationshipMatrices.jl Pedigree validation, A, A⁻¹, G, H⁻¹, kinship and realized IBD.\nBreeding.jl (Phase 4 dependency; see §4.6.) Mixed-model equations and solvers, variance components (REML, Gibbs), GBLUP/Bayesian regression, PEV and reliability, optimum-contribution selection (TM1997, OC_fixsex2), and robust truncation and contribution selection (rocs: TS, RTS, OCS, ROCS; Fogg et al. 2026).\nBnGsim.jl Scenario definitions, founder sampling, meiosis, QTL and phenotype simulation, lifecycle, choosing which selection and mating policy runs when, results, checkpoints and the CLI.\nbngsim-service A separate application repository for the web UI/API, the PostgreSQL metadata store, the job protocol and the private worker. It runs the BnGsim CLI as a subprocess; it does not import BnGsim.\n\nAlphaSimR and MoBPS show how to build flexible breeding programs from scenarios, and QMSim and SLiM serve as research references. None of them is a dependency, and BnGsim does not aim for API compatibility with them. Sequence-level forward simulation belongs in FisherWright (or SLiM), not here.","title":"1. Purpose and package boundaries"},{"category":"section","location":"roadmap/#2.-Current-status-(v0.4.0)","page":"Roadmap","text":"Area State\nFounders historical_population/HistoricalSpec wrap fisher_wright. continue_population extends a run. founder_haplotypes samples founders from a FisherWrightResult, a packed BNGH1 file, or xyBnG BitMatrix output. sample_founder_population returns a FounderPopulation built on VariantMap and LocusSet.\nFounder diagnostics founder_summary (SFS, LD decay, chip/QTL MAF, VanRaden G statistics, implied Mₑ), ld_decay, vanraden_g.\nKeyed streams SimKey, keyed_rng (Philox4x64-10 → Xoshiro), golden test (§4.2).\nMeiosis MeiosisMap, make_progeny! (keyed, word-wise, per-chromosome genetic lengths, MeiosisRecord); tested against exact theory. The older crossovers, gamete!, gene_drop! with an explicit rng. random_mate, rnd_ped, expand.\nSampling sample_id, sample_loci (MAF-filtered chip and QTL LocusSets).\nTraits eqtl (effects scaled only, mean in the intercept, each trait's da honoured, realised covariance exact in the base population), trait_summary, tbv!, phenotype! (correlated residuals, sex-limited expression, threshold traits from a liability scale), dummy_traits.\nProvenance provenance (Breeding is listed when loaded) writes a text header with the date, host, CPU, threads, seed and package versions/commits. This partly covers I2: there is no JSON manifest yet.\nBreeding loop Population (ids equal rows; nothing is removed), Scheme, step!/simulate!: discrete or overlapping generations (parents up to a maximum age), one or several additive traits with sex- and age-limited records, selection on an index of multi-trait EBVs, optional (multi-trait) REML re-estimation of the covariance components, survival and culling, single-step GBLUP with a genotyped window, OCS with a target ΔF per generation for overlapping generations (newborn-cohort, age-weighted per year, age-weighted per generation), crossover records kept for realized IBD from founder segments (founder_segments, RelationshipMatrices 0.7.1), checkpoints that resume a run identically, evaluation through Breeding.jl (:tbv, :phenotype, :pblup, :gblup, :ssgblup), optionally on a window of recent cohorts and their traced ancestors through a PedigreeView (id ↔ row, topological, 0 for parents outside), selection by truncation, TM1997, rocs or at random, (criterion, id) ranking, keyed pairing and meiosis, per-generation summaries.\nScenarios A versioned JSON scenario (Scenario, schema \"bngsim-scenario\" version 1, 0.13.0): species, historical population and founders, QTL and chip panels, traits, scheme, steps, seed, replicates. Reading and validation that report every problem with its path (unknown, repeated and mistyped keys, ranges, combinations), a canonical form that survives a JSON round trip, and run_scenario/run_replicate, keyed so that a replicate does not depend on the others or on threads.\nCommand line `bin/bngsim validate\nResource estimate resource_estimate / bngsim estimate (0.15.0): expected peak memory from a component model (process, historical population, genomes, dense evaluation; sizes from neutral theory), fitted on 16 scenarios and checked on 6 held out (every mean within 15 %; examples/minimal.json within 10 %), a bound of 1.3 × the expectation that no run exceeded, disk, and a rough run time; run --memory-limit refuses a scenario whose bound exceeds it (exit code 5). Calibrated on one host; scripts in validation/estimate/.\nResume bngsim resume (0.16.0): a file DIR/CANCEL stops a run after the current step with its state saved (exit code 7), --checkpoint-every K saves it periodically, and resume DIR continues; outputs byte-identical to an uninterrupted run, every session in the provenance.\nArchives bngsim run --archive none|chip|qtl|all and write_archive (0.17.0): pedigree, records, true and estimated breeding values, traits, QTL, and PLINK 1 binary genotypes (checked with PLINK 1.9 and plink2), per replicate, in the provenance; infinitesimal draws exact N(0, G0 ⊗ A) breeding values and phenotypes on a pedigree (Tier 2 outputs of Phase 5, for the benchmark of roadmap §7a).\nAdapter layer src/adapters/ (0.18.0): FisherWright, RelationshipMatrices and Breeding are imported only through one module each, which re-exports the names BnGsim uses and absorbs version differences (census, dense_haplotypes); a layering test forbids any other reference, and contract tests pin the conventions BnGsim relies on (docs page \"Upstream contract\"). This closes Phases 0–1.\nAggregation aggregate.csv from every run and bngsim merge (0.19.0): per step, mean, SD and SE over replicates of every summary and of the per-step gain (dG) and rate of inbreeding (dF); runs of disjoint replicates (on several hosts) merge into the bytes of one run, whatever the order. Tier 1 summaries stay CSV (no Parquet dependency).\nReports (0.21.0) per cohort, the QTL at which the favourable allele is segregating, fixed or lost (qtl_seg, qtl_fixed, qtl_lost), and the realized selection intensity of each sex (intensity_sire, intensity_dam: the parents' mean index weighted by offspring, less the eligible candidates' mean, in their SDs); in aggregate.csv, variance depletion (var_ratio, genic_ratio, relative to the first step) and dFhet. Already reported: ΔG, accuracy, generation interval, pedigree F and coancestry, Fhet, realized IBD (Fseg). 0.22.0 completes the list: Fgrm (VanRaden G with founder frequencies; E ≈ F − (1 − F)/(2n₀ − 1) without selection, checked over 200 replicates) and, with report_reliability, the candidates' mean reliability 1 − PEV/σ²a from the MME (solved in chunks of 64 candidates; checked against a dense inverse).\nSignals SIGINT and SIGTERM to bin/bngsim (0.20.0) create DIR/CANCEL, so the run stops at the next step boundary with its state saved (exit code 7); before any state exists, or on a second signal, Julia is stopped at once. Handled by the launcher, not by Julia, whose delivery of interrupts to threaded tasks differs between versions. Resumed runs are byte-identical.\nNot planned removal of animals' records from the store (decided 2026-11-23): a record is about 20 + 24k bytes (k traits), 44 MB per million animals with one trait, while genomes, the large part, are already dropped once unusable (retain = :active, 0.12.0); pedigree F and coancestry need every ancestor anyway. Ids stay rows of the store. Per-step work over the whole store is kept to cheap passes (0.23.0: :random selection draws only for eligible candidates, same values).\n\nThe breeding loop is keyed by coordinate (§4.2): meiosis, sexes, phenotypes, random selection and pairing each draw from their own SimKey, and integers are sampled from raw 64-bit outputs (keyed_int), so a run is identical at any thread count and on Julia 1.10 and 1.13. The older building blocks still take an explicit rng.","title":"2. Current status (v0.4.0)"},{"category":"section","location":"roadmap/#3.-Design-invariants","page":"Roadmap","text":"I1 — Bitwise reproducibility. With a pinned Julia version, manifest, CPU architecture and platform, a manifest and seed reproduce Tier 1 results bit for bit, whatever the thread count, process count, or checkpoint/resume history.\nI1a — Deterministic decisions. Every discrete decision derived from a floating-point quantity uses a total order with an explicit tie-break on ascending id. Selection, mating assignment and culling sort by (criterion, id).\nI1b — Scoped cross-platform claim. Across platforms, BnGsim claims identical discrete decisions only up to the first decision that uses a solver result. After that it claims statistical equivalence over replicates, never a tolerance on a single run. A 1-ulp change in an EBV can select a different animal, and from then on it is a different simulation.\nI2 — Immutable provenance. Every output records the scenario JSON and its schema version, the BnGsim version, the manifest hash, the Julia version, OS/CPU, the worker identity, and the seed.\nI3 — Explicit failure. No event silently drops animals, truncates candidate pools, repairs invalid values, or marks a partial result as successful.\nI4 — Identity stability. Animal IDs are Int32, unique per replicate, and never reused or treated as row positions.\nI5 — One coordinate system. Markers, QTL, chip loci and map positions all resolve through one ordered VariantMap.\nI6 — Pedigree monotonicity. Parents exist before their offspring. Any dense row indexing that RelationshipMatrices or Breeding needs is built in an adapter and never exposed as an identity.\nI7 — Bounded footprint. Memory, disk, CPU and wall time are estimated at admission and enforced at run time.\nI8 — Single execution path. Tests, local CLI runs, web validation and worker runs all execute the same code path.\nI9 — Zero inbound attack surface. The compute machine has no public listener and accepts no user code, shell commands, paths or worker flags.","title":"3. Design invariants"},{"category":"section","location":"roadmap/#4.-Architectural-decisions","page":"Roadmap","text":"","title":"4. Architectural decisions"},{"category":"section","location":"roadmap/#4.1-Upstream-contract-and-adapter-layer","page":"Roadmap","text":"Before Phase 0 code depends on upstream internals, write docs/upstream-contract.md. It records the exported API, the packing and ploidy layout, the missing-parent sentinel (0), allele coding and index base for each dependency. All upstream calls go through src/adapters/, one thin module per dependency, each with a contract test. Serialized manifests and result tables use types that BnGsim owns.\n\nAs of v0.2.0 BnGsim calls no unexported FisherWright function: continue_population is fisher_wright(...; init = res, popsize = ns).","title":"4.1 Upstream contract and adapter layer"},{"category":"section","location":"roadmap/#4.2-Counter-based-keyed-PRNG-(SimKey)","page":"Roadmap","text":"struct SimKey\n    seed::UInt64        # scenario master seed\n    replicate::UInt32\n    generation::UInt32\n    domain::UInt16      # FOUNDERS, MEIOSIS, PHENOTYPE, MATING, SURVIVAL, …\n    stage::UInt16       # stable named event code, never a schedule index\n    entity::UInt32      # 0 when population-scoped\n    subcounter::UInt64\nend\n\nEach draw is a pure function of its coordinate. The key is mapped onto a counter-based generator (Philox/Threefry) with a documented bit-packing: key = (seed, replicate), counter = (generation, domain, stage, entity, subcounter). Base.hash and objectid are prohibited on this path. A golden test hard-codes the outputs for fixed keys.\n\nProgeny draws are keyed by (pair_index, within_pair_index) from the mating plan, not by the offspring id. IDs are then assigned in that same order, so they do not depend on the thread schedule. Draws for animals that already exist (phenotype, survival, genotyping) are keyed by id.","title":"4.2 Counter-based keyed PRNG (SimKey)"},{"category":"section","location":"roadmap/#4.3-State-storage-and-the-ID/row-boundary","page":"Roadmap","text":"Hot-loop state lives in a typed structure of arrays (IndividualStore: Vector{Int32}, Vector{Float64}, BitVector). DataFrames are projections for output only. PedigreeView holds id_to_row/row_to_id and the 1-based topological order with 0 for unknown parents. A property test checks the round trip row_to_id(id_to_row(x)) == x on pruned, sparse and reordered populations.\n\nHaplotypes are stored in generation-sized blocks. Two retention policies exist (0.12.0): full (retain = :all), and active-only (retain = :active), which drops the genomes no later step can use (dead, culled or too old to breed, and not genotyped) but keeps every animal's pedigree and records; a cohort's block is compacted to the genomes kept.","title":"4.3 State storage and the ID/row boundary"},{"category":"section","location":"roadmap/#4.4-Meiosis-ownership","page":"Roadmap","text":"BnGsim decides who and when: the crossover model, map function, interference, sex-specific maps and parent choice. The packed-genome layer decides how bits move. In the current code, gamete! copies bit segments on packed Haplotypes. The target interface is\n\noffspring = make_progeny(parents, mating_plan, meiosis_model, key_base)\n\nwhere key_base is a SimKey prefix, not a shared stream. The result is then the same however the plan is split across threads.","title":"4.4 Meiosis ownership"},{"category":"section","location":"roadmap/#4.5-Reproduction-abstraction","page":"Roadmap","text":"ReproductionModel is declared in Phase 1 with outcrossing implemented. Selfing, doubled haploids, clonal propagation and full-sib lines are declared as variants now, so that plant schemes, where families or lines rather than individuals are selected, do not force a rewrite.","title":"4.5 Reproduction abstraction"},{"category":"section","location":"roadmap/#4.6-Evaluation-and-contribution-selection-are-delegated","page":"Roadmap","text":"BnGsim builds the evaluation inputs (records, pedigree projection, genotype matrix) and calls Breeding.jl to solve. It does not have its own MME solvers. Optimum-contribution and robust selection policies call Breeding.TM1997, Breeding.OC_fixsex2 or Breeding.rocs. rocs takes the PEV/PEC matrix Ω from Breeding's lhs_inverse/pev and the relationship matrix Σ (A, G, H or I), so one call covers TS, RTS, OCS and ROCS.\n\nJulia support: BnGsim supports Julia 1.10 (LTS) through the current release. In v0.1 Breeding is not a dependency, and provenance reports it only if the session has loaded it. Breeding lowered its floor to Julia 1.10 in v0.2.1, so Phase 4 can add Breeding = \"0.2.1\" without raising BnGsim's Julia floor.","title":"4.6 Evaluation and contribution selection are delegated"},{"category":"section","location":"roadmap/#4.7-CLI-first-boundary-and-layering","page":"Roadmap","text":"BnGsim.jl is the registered package. It holds the core, results, checkpoints and the CLI. Heavy optional dependencies (Parquet, plotting) go in ext/ as package extensions. The web service is a separate repository that calls the CLI.\n\nThe submodules form a layered DAG. Back edges between layers are not allowed, and CI enforces this:\n\nLayer May import from\nL0 adapters/, config/ nothing in BnGsim (and not each other)\nL1 state/ L0\nL2 genetics/ L0–L1\nL3 breeding/, evaluate/ L0–L2\nL4 results/ L0–L3\nL5 cli/ L0–L4","title":"4.7 CLI-first boundary and layering"},{"category":"section","location":"roadmap/#4.8-The-CLI-is-versioned-public-API","page":"Roadmap","text":"bngsim validate manifest.json   # schema + policy, no execution\nbngsim estimate manifest.json   # predicted peak RAM, disk, runtime\nbngsim run      manifest.json\nbngsim resume   checkpoint.dir\nbngsim version  --json          # package version, CLI interface version, manifest hash\n\nExit codes are enumerated and stable. They distinguish success, invalid manifest, policy/resource rejection, budget exceeded, controlled simulation failure, cancellation at a checkpoint, and internal error. Structured output goes to stdout and diagnostics to stderr. The CLI interface version is separate from the package version. A Tier 1 contract test covers the arguments, exit codes and output shape.","title":"4.8 The CLI is versioned public API"},{"category":"section","location":"roadmap/#5.-Capacity-model","page":"Roadmap","text":"For diploid packed genomes (1 bit per allele):\n\nhaplotype bytes   = n_retained × n_loci / 4\ndense G bytes     = 8 × n_genotyped²\nsparse A⁻¹ nnz    ≈ 10–16 × n_pedigree\n\nScenario Loci Retained Haplotypes Dense G Note\nUnit test 2 000 500 0.25 MB 2 MB CI, < 10 s\nSmoke / tutorial 20 000 10 000 50 MB 800 MB laptop\nRealistic cattle 50 000 100 000 1.25 GB 20 GB (50 000 genotyped) dense G is the wall\nLarge study 100 000 500 000 12.5 GB infeasible reference-set pruning, APY, iterative solver in Breeding\n\nThe scaling bottleneck is n_genotyped, not haplotype storage. bngsim estimate does this arithmetic at admission, and the runtime fails cleanly before the OS kills the process.","title":"5. Capacity model"},{"category":"section","location":"roadmap/#6.-Phases","page":"Roadmap","text":"Each phase ends with an exit gate. Phases 2 and 3 are partly done (see §2).","title":"6. Phases"},{"category":"section","location":"roadmap/#Phase-0-—-Executable-vertical-slice","page":"Roadmap","text":"CLI skeleton, SimKey, and a JSON provenance block that extends the current provenance header.\ndocs/upstream-contract.md and the first src/adapters/ modules. (The FisherWright-internal calls were removed in v0.2.0.)\nA minimal scenario: small genome, one additive trait, random mating, phenotypic truncation, 3 generations.\nA benchmark harness on the smoke scenario, with a recorded baseline.\n\nGate: bngsim run examples/minimal.json finishes in under 10 s and produces byte-identical Tier 1 output at 1 and 8 threads. The SimKey golden test passes.","title":"Phase 0 — Executable vertical slice"},{"category":"section","location":"roadmap/#Phase-1-—-Scenario-contracts-and-state","page":"Roadmap","text":"A versioned SimulationConfig JSON schema. It validates parameter bounds, locus ordering, QTL/chip overlap policy, trait dimensions and event schedules.\nIndividualStore, PedigreeView, and the ID/row property tests.\nThe ReproductionModel and MeiosisModel extension points.\nbngsim validate, estimate, version --json, and the exit-code table.\n\nGate: the config survives a JSON round trip unchanged. About 30 malformed manifests are rejected with explicit errors. estimate is within ±25% of the measured peak RAM on the smoke scenario. The CLI contract test passes. (0.15.0: the ±25% applies to the expected peak, which is also checked on 21 other scenarios; the bound used for admission is never below a measured run. The garbage collector alone moves the peak of one scenario by up to 25% between runs, so the gate is checked on the mean of two.)","title":"Phase 1 — Scenario contracts and state"},{"category":"section","location":"roadmap/#Phase-2-—-Founders,-genomic-architecture,-traits-*(partly-done)*","page":"Roadmap","text":"Founders come only from fisher_wright. Record the burn-in config, base frequencies, LD decay and genetic variance; founder_summary already computes most of these.\nChip LocusSets and QTL architectures in one VariantMap. Done: sample_loci, eqtl.\nQTL effect distributions: Gaussian, Gamma, Laplace, Gamma with random sign, spike-and-slab (done, 0.23.0: scenario families and the samplers RandomSign, SpikeSlab).\nPhenotypes: TBV plus fixed/contemporary-group effects plus correlated residuals. Done: correlated residuals. To do: contemporary groups.\nThreshold traits (done), sex-limited traits (done), age-limited and repeated records (to do), missing-record masks.\nFounder histories with selection or demography, using the FisherWright 0.6 FitnessLoci/TraitLoci, bottleneck and growth.\n\nGate: realized Var(TBV) and the trait correlations recover their targets within pre-declared sampling tolerances. LD decays monotonically. The neutral SFS matches its expectation.","title":"Phase 2 — Founders, genomic architecture, traits (partly done)"},{"category":"section","location":"roadmap/#Phase-3-—-Meiosis,-lifecycle,-mating-*(partly-done)*","page":"Roadmap","text":"MeiosisModel: Poisson/Haldane (done), Gamma-count interference, sex-specific maps.\nDemography: age classes, parity, survival, reproductive limits, culling, and discrete or overlapping generations.\nMating: random (done), assortative/disassortative, minimum-coancestry, factorial, fixed-progeny.\nStrict validation that parents are appended before offspring.\n\nGate: crossover counts match the map length in Morgans. Parent–offspring realized IBD ≈ 0.5. Mendelian sampling variance mathrmVar(mathrmMS) approx tfrac12(1-bar F)sigma_A^2. Pedigree A and realized IBD agree in expectation. Phase forms contiguous parental segments. Allele dosage is conserved without selection.","title":"Phase 3 — Meiosis, lifecycle, mating (partly done)"},{"category":"section","location":"roadmap/#Phase-4-—-Evaluation-and-selection-(through-Breeding.jl)","page":"Roadmap","text":"Evaluations, in this order: TBV oracle → phenotypic truncation → PBLUP (A⁻¹) → GBLUP (G from packed haplotypes: base frequencies, MAF filter, blending G_w = (1-w)G + wA_22) → ssGBLUP (H⁻¹). Solving happens in Breeding.jl.\nMulti-trait economic indices, sex-specific selection intensity, multi-stage selection.\nContribution policies: truncation, TM1997 (target coancestry), rocs (TS/RTS/OCS/ROCS with Ω from PEV).\n(criterion, id) ranking everywhere (I1a). Per-animal PEV and reliability R^2 = 1 - mathrmPEVsigma_a^2.\n\nGate:\n\nThe TBV oracle gives ρ = 1 by construction.\nPBLUP agrees with an independent MME solve on a small data set.\nGBLUP and ssGBLUP agree when everyone is genotyped and w = 0.\nρ(phenotype) < ρ(PBLUP) < ρ(GBLUP) ≤ ρ(ssGBLUP) on a named scenario built so that this should hold (dense panel, large reference). The replicate count is declared, and the margin must exceed the Monte-Carlo SE.\nrocs with κ = λ = 0 reproduces truncation selection.\nSelection does not change with thread count on a scenario with deliberate ties.","title":"Phase 4 — Evaluation and selection (through Breeding.jl)"},{"category":"section","location":"roadmap/#Phase-5-—-Persistence,-checkpoints,-replicates","page":"Roadmap","text":"Tier 1 compact summaries (JSON/Parquet) and Tier 2 archives (pedigree, EBVs, haplotypes).\nReports: ΔG, variance depletion, accuracy, PEV/reliability, intensity, generation interval, ΔF (pedigree and genomic), realized IBD, fixation and loss.\nReplicate parallelism with aggregation that does not depend on order.\nGeneration-boundary checkpoints, SIGINT/SIGTERM handling, and bngsim resume as the only way to resume.\n\nGate: killing a run and then running bngsim resume gives output byte-identical to an uninterrupted run. Aggregates do not depend on the order in which replicates finish.","title":"Phase 5 — Persistence, checkpoints, replicates"},{"category":"section","location":"roadmap/#Phase-6-—-Web-service-(bngsim-service)","page":"Roadmap","text":"Scenario builder and submission UI that runs validate/estimate preflight.\nA private worker that polls outbound over HTTPS/mTLS, takes atomic leases, runs under cgroup limits as a non-root user, and calls bngsim run.\nJob state machine: queued → claimed → running → completed | failed | cancelled. A lease that expires goes back to queued or ends as lost. Terminal state comes only from the CLI exit code, and a job is never marked completed for a worker that stopped reporting.\nArtifacts are uploaded idempotently under content-addressed keys, with retention classes, quota GC, schema migrations and user-safe error messages.\n\nGate: all acceptance scenarios (§8) pass with no inbound access to the worker, against a released and pinned BnGsim. BnGsim.jl itself gains no HTTP, database or auth dependency.","title":"Phase 6 — Web service (bngsim-service)"},{"category":"section","location":"roadmap/#7.-Verification-tiers","page":"Roadmap","text":"Tier Trigger Content\n1 — invariants and contracts every commit, seconds, zero flakiness schema round trip, ID/row bijection, pedigree monotonicity, thread invariance (1 vs 8), checkpoint/resume identity, adapter and CLI contracts, SimKey golden streams, layering, tie determinism\n2 — statistical nightly, minutes, pre-registered tolerances breeder's equation Delta G = i r_IH sigma_A, Bulmer effect, neutral Delta F approx 1(2N_e), Mendelian sampling variance, crossover rate vs map length, pedigree vs realized IBD\n3 — regression and cross-tool releases, hours golden scenario hashes, migration fixtures, qualitative trends vs AlphaSimR/QMSim, statistical (not run-level) cross-platform equivalence\n\nA change to a golden hash is never accepted as a plain test update. It needs a reviewed diff that explains which decision changed and why.","title":"7. Verification tiers"},{"category":"section","location":"roadmap/#8.-Acceptance-scenarios","page":"Roadmap","text":"On a pinned platform, two runs with the same seed give byte-identical Tier 1 output at 1, 2 and 8 threads, including a scenario with deliberate EBV ties.\nInvalid parameters, coordinate mismatches and oversized resource requests are rejected before a job is created.\nThe worker claims a job through outbound HTTPS, runs a 20-generation ssGBLUP scenario, and uploads the results.\nThe UI shows the state transitions and generation progress, and offers the Tier 1 metrics and the manifest for download.\nResubmitting the downloaded manifest reproduces the run.\nWhen a worker crashes, its lease expires and the job is requeued or marked lost, never completed.\nA cancelled job stops at a generation boundary, and bngsim resume continues it to a byte-identical completion.\nAll Tier 2 tests pass nightly within their declared tolerances.","title":"8. Acceptance scenarios"},{"category":"section","location":"roadmap/#9.-Deferred-scope","page":"Roadmap","text":"Feature Re-entry trigger\nIterative (PCG) / APY evaluation n_genotyped pushes a direct solve past the worker RAM budget (§5). The work goes in Breeding.jl.\nDominance, heterosis, epistasis Additive validation is complete and a study needs non-additive variance.\nPlant reproduction (selfing, DH, clones) A concrete line/family workflow is scheduled.\nLive UI (SSE/WebSocket) Polling measurably degrades the user experience.\nSlurm / HPC worker Single-worker throughput is a measured bottleneck.\nZarr / multidimensional storage Parquet Tier 2 output is a measured I/O bottleneck.\n\nOptimum-contribution selection is no longer deferred. Breeding.jl provides the solvers (§4.6), so BnGsim only has to wire them in as a selection policy in Phase 4.","title":"9. Deferred scope"},{"category":"section","location":"roadmap/#10.-Risk-register","page":"Roadmap","text":"Risk Impact Mitigation\nAPI churn across four sibling packages High §4.1 contract document and adapters; tight [compat] bounds; remove calls to unexported internals.\nNon-reproducibility under threads or resume High SimKey (§4.2); thread-invariance and resume-identity tests.\nFloating-point tie divergence High I1a (criterion, id) ordering; I1b scoped claim; deliberate-ties gate.\nUnspecified CLI contract leading to wrong job status High §4.8 enumerated exit codes; Tier 1 CLI test.\nMeiosis keys that depend on creation order High Key progeny by (pair_index, within_pair_index) (§4.2).\nID/row conflation corrupting A/G High PedigreeView; round-trip property tests.\nDense G memory wall Medium §5 capacity model; bngsim estimate admission.\nFlaky statistical tests getting muted Medium Tier 2 kept separate, with pre-registered tolerances and fixed seeds.\nDuplicated evaluation code in BnGsim and Breeding Medium §4.6: BnGsim never solves MMEs.\nJulia floor mismatch with Breeding Resolved Breeding 0.2.1 supports Julia 1.10 (§4.6); CI covers 1.10 and the current release.\nScope inflation Medium Phase 0 vertical slice first; §9 re-entry triggers.\nWeb dependencies leaking into the core Medium CLI subprocess boundary; Phase 6 gate.\nDisk exhaustion on the worker Medium Tiered artifacts, retention classes, quotas, GC.","title":"10. Risk register"},{"category":"section","location":"estimate/#Resource-estimate","page":"Resource estimate","text":"resource_estimate and bngsim estimate predict, from the scenario alone, the peak resident memory of a run, a conservative bound on it, the disk the command line writes, and a rough run time. bngsim run --memory-limit SIZE refuses a scenario whose bound exceeds SIZE before it starts (exit code 5), so that a run fails cleanly instead of being killed by the operating system (roadmap invariant I7).","title":"Resource estimate"},{"category":"section","location":"estimate/#The-model","page":"Resource estimate","text":"Peak memory is the sum of four terms, each a fitted multiple of a size computed from the scenario (ScenarioSize):\n\nTerm Size Fitted bytes per byte\nprocess BnGsim loaded and compiled 577 MB\nhistorical population one copy of its haplotypes, 8Nbar m with bar m = mu L min(t 4N) derived alleles per haplotype 5.42\ngenomes the packed genomes of all animals, 2 cdot n_animals cdot S  8, with S = 4Nmu L ln(1 + 2 n_id t  4N) segregating loci among the founders 4.15\ndense evaluation one n_g times n_g matrix, 8 n_g^2, for the genotyped animals 13.3 (:gblup), 19.0 (:ssgblup)\n\nbar m and S follow from neutral theory for a population that starts without variation: mu L new alleles per haplotype per generation until fixation balances them near 4Nmu L, and the total branch length of the founders' genealogy truncated at the start of the run. On the calibration scenarios they matched the realized values within 4 %. The multiples count the copies the code holds at its peak (buffers of the historical run; the relationship matrix, its blend with A, its factor and inverse and the mixed-model equations of GBLUP), plus memory not yet collected. Terms for the pedigree and for the dense cohort matrix of the per-step summary were tried and fitted to zero.\n\nThe bound is the expected peak times 1.3: no calibration or held-out run exceeded the expectation by more than 15.3 %.\n\nRun time is the alleles carried through meiosis in the historical run (about 2Nmu L sum_g min(g 4N)), plus, per step, terms in the evaluated pedigree and the cube of the genotyped set, plus a fixed cost of the dense evaluations. It is a rough guide: within a factor of about two on the calibration host.","title":"The model"},{"category":"section","location":"estimate/#Calibration","page":"Resource estimate","text":"The coefficients were fitted on one host: AMD Threadripper 3970X, Linux x86_64, Julia 1.13.4, 8 threads, BnGsim 0.14. Sixteen calibration scenarios cover the historical population (N 500 to 3,000, t 500 to 4,000), PBLUP with 6,000 to 41,000 animals, and GBLUP and ssGBLUP with 2,000 to 8,000 genotyped animals; six held-out scenarios, not used in the fit, vary the genome, the number of traits, the genotyped cohorts and the historical size, and include examples/minimal.json. Each ran twice in a fresh process. The garbage collector makes the peak of one scenario vary between runs by up to 25 % (GBLUP with 8,000 animals: 4,512 and 5,761 MB), so the expectation is fitted to the mean of the two runs.\n\n  baseline       5.76692e+08\n  history        5.41563\n  genomes        4.14973\n  pedigree       0\n  cohort         0\n  dense_gblup    13.2754\n  dense_ssgblup  19.0071\n\nscenario           runs (MB)              expected (MB)   rel. error of mean   max ratio\n  gblup_2000.json  1040, 1117                  969    +11.4%   1.153 \n  gblup_4000.json  1736, 1653                 1861     -8.9%   0.933 \n  gblup_6000.json  3579, 3421                 3271     +7.0%   1.094 \n  gblup_8000.json  4512, 5761                 5200     -1.2%   1.108 \n  h1000_1000.json  606, 595                    607     -1.1%   0.999 \n  h2000_2000.json  764, 748                    746     +1.3%   1.024 \n  h2000_4000.json  928, 943                    915     +2.3%   1.031 \n  h3000_3000.json  958, 910                    968     -3.5%   0.990 \n  h500_500.json    533, 534                    568     -6.1%   0.940 \n  p1000_5.json     779, 816                    739     +7.9%   1.105 \n  p2000_10.json    1133, 1124                 1108     +1.9%   1.023 \n  p4000_10.json    1553, 1544                 1600     -3.2%   0.971 \n  ssgblup_2000.json 1131, 1079                 1110     -0.5%   1.018 \n  ssgblup_4000.json 2151, 2508                 2366     -1.5%   1.060 \n  ssgblup_6000.json 4710, 4642                 4364     +7.1%   1.079 \n  ssgblup_8000.json 6871, 6774                 7105     -4.0%   0.967 \n  v1.json          1539, 1572                 1720     -9.6%   0.914 (held out)\n  v2.json          3542, 2745                 3433     -8.4%   1.032 (held out)\n  v3.json          4830, 4904                 4610     +5.6%   1.064 (held out)\n  v4.json          1132, 1118                  985    +14.3%   1.150 (held out)\n  v5.json          494, 499                    551     -9.8%   0.907 (held out)\n  v6.json          1217, 1231                 1272     -3.8%   0.967 (held out)\n\nlargest measured/expected over all runs: 1.153\ntime coefficients [7.631442530977135e-10, 1.138841810563798e-5, 8.445648775015476e-12, 4.4804669798398145]\nratio measured/model for runs over 1 s: 0.75 to 2.13\n  gblup_2000.json  measured   4.2 s  model   5.6 s\n  gblup_4000.json  measured  14.1 s  model   7.1 s\n  gblup_6000.json  measured  10.2 s  model  10.8 s\n  gblup_8000.json  measured  14.1 s  model  17.8 s\n  h1000_1000.json  measured   0.9 s  model   0.8 s\n  h2000_2000.json  measured   5.1 s  model   6.1 s\n  h2000_4000.json  measured  20.0 s  model  24.4 s\n  h3000_3000.json  measured  21.1 s  model  20.6 s\n  h500_500.json    measured   0.2 s  model   0.1 s\n  p1000_5.json     measured   1.6 s  model   1.0 s\n  p2000_10.json    measured   3.2 s  model   2.9 s\n  p4000_10.json    measured   4.3 s  model   5.0 s\n  ssgblup_2000.json measured   7.1 s  model   5.6 s\n  ssgblup_4000.json measured   8.9 s  model   7.1 s\n  ssgblup_6000.json measured  11.1 s  model  10.8 s\n  ssgblup_8000.json measured  27.8 s  model  17.8 s\n  v1.json          measured  11.5 s  model  12.3 s\n  v2.json          measured  12.0 s  model  13.6 s\n  v3.json          measured  21.9 s  model  12.5 s\n  v4.json          measured   3.6 s  model   1.7 s\n  v5.json          measured   0.1 s  model   0.0 s\n  v6.json          measured  37.4 s  model  48.9 s\n\n\n\n\nThe mean of every scenario lies within 15 % of its estimate (calibration within 11.4 %, held out within 14.3 %), and examples/minimal.json, the roadmap's smoke scenario, within 10 %. The test suite checks the roadmap's gate on these measurements: every mean within 25 % of the estimate and no run above the bound.\n\nOn another host, Julia version or thread count the coefficients change: on a 12-core desktop (AMD Ryzen 9 3900X) at 4 threads the same scenarios peaked 6 % to 28 % lower than on the calibration host at 8 (GBLUP with 6,000 genotyped animals: 2,536 MB against about 3,500 MB), so there the estimate errs high, on the safe side. The scripts to measure and refit are in validation/estimate/ (run.sh, measure.jl, fit.jl).","title":"Calibration"},{"category":"section","location":"archive/#Archives","page":"Archives","text":"An archive holds what other software needs to analyse a simulated population: the pedigree, the records, the true and estimated breeding values, the QTL with their effects, and genotypes. bngsim run ... --archive PANEL writes one per replicate to DIR/archive/r<N>/ when the replicate finishes, and write_archive writes one from Julia.\n\nFile Content\npedigree.csv id, sire, dam, sex, generation, exit; 0 for an unknown parent, sex M/F, exit the time step the animal died or was culled (empty while alive)\nrecords.csv id and one column per trait: the phenotype where it was recorded (expressed in the animal's sex, at or after the trait's age, alive then), empty otherwise\ntruth.csv id, tbv_<trait>..., ebv_<trait>...: the true genetic values and the last evaluation\ntraits.csv per trait: name, h2, sigma_a, mu, sex, age, intercept\nqtl.csv locus, chr, bp (within the chromosome) and the effect of one copy of the allele coded 1 for each trait\ngenotypes.bed/.bim/.fam PLINK 1 binary genotypes of every animal whose genome is kept, at the loci of PANEL\n\nPANEL is chip (the scenario's chip), qtl, all (every segregating locus of the founders) or none (no genotypes). The archives are listed with their SHA-256 in provenance.json, and a stopped and resumed run writes the same files, byte for byte.\n\nIn genotypes.bed the allele coded 1 is A1, the fifth column of genotypes.bim. PLINK 1.9 counts it with --recode A --keep-allele-order; without that flag it recodes A1 as the minor allele of the sample. plink2 counts it with --export A --export-allele on that column, and by default counts the sixth. Both tools were checked to read back the simulated genotypes exactly (200 animals, 300 loci).","title":"Archives"},{"category":"section","location":"archive/#Infinitesimal-breeding-values","page":"Archives","text":"The breeding loop simulates finite loci. To test software on data whose genetic model is exactly the animal model, infinitesimal draws u sim N(0 G_0 otimes A) and phenotypes y = mu + u + e, e sim N(0 R_0), on any pedigree, by Mendelian sampling: each animal gets the mean of its parents' values plus a term with covariance (1 - tfrac14(1 + F_s) - tfrac14(1 + F_d)) G_0. Every animal draws from its own SimKey stream, so its values do not depend on the order of computation. The test suite checks the covariances against G_0 otimes A on a pedigree with inbred animals.\n\nusing BnGsim\nr = run_replicate(read_scenario(\"examples/minimal.json\"))\npop = r.population\nu, y = infinitesimal(pop.sire, pop.dam, [1.0;;], [3.0;;], SimKey(1))","title":"Infinitesimal breeding values"},{"category":"section","location":"upstream-contract/#Upstream-contract","page":"Upstream contract","text":"BnGsim builds on three JuliaBnG packages besides the shared types of BnGStructs: FisherWright (historical populations), RelationshipMatrices (relationship matrices and realized IBD) and Breeding (evaluation and selection). It imports each of them in one place only, an adapter module in src/adapters/ that re-exports the names BnGsim uses and absorbs differences between their versions. A test (test/adapters.jl, \"Upstream contract: layering\") fails if any other source file imports or names them, and the contract tests below fail if a version of an upstream package breaks a convention BnGsim relies on. BnGStructs is the shared vocabulary of all packages (Haplotype, VariantMap, Species, TraitQTL, SNPSet, LocusSet) and is used directly. Result tables and files that BnGsim writes use types and formats BnGsim owns (CSV, JSON, PLINK).","title":"Upstream contract"},{"category":"section","location":"upstream-contract/#FisherWright-(FisherWrightAdapter)","page":"Upstream contract","text":"Names: fisher_wright, FisherWrightResult, muts2bitarray, and census, dense_haplotypes (version-independent).\n\nA population of N diploids is 2N haplotypes; individual i is haplotypes 2i - 1 and 2i.\nA haplotype is a sorted, unique Vector{UInt32} of the positions of the derived alleles it carries: genome-wide, 1-based coordinates on the chromosomes laid end to end (the VariantMap convention of BnGStructs).\nchromosome_ends is cumsum of the chromosome lengths, as UInt32; substitutions are the sorted positions fixed and removed.\nA run with the same rng gives the same population; init with popsize continues a run and appends to its census record.\ncensus(res): the census sizes N₀, N₁, … (res.records.census from FisherWright 0.10, res.census before).\ndense_haplotypes(muts, ends; rng): the loci segregating among muts as a BitMatrix (loci × haplotypes) and a map with chr (1-based), genome-wide pos and the allele frequency frq; the placeholder nucleotides come from rng from FisherWright 0.10 on (BnGsim passes a copy, so the caller's stream does not advance), from the global generator before.\nThe species method reads the genetic length of each chromosome from Species.morgan (FisherWright 0.8.2 and later).","title":"FisherWright (FisherWrightAdapter)"},{"category":"section","location":"upstream-contract/#RelationshipMatrices-(RelationshipsAdapter)","page":"Upstream contract","text":"Names: nrm, nrm_diag, ainv, grm, hinv, irm, Meioses, HaplotypeSegments, founder_segments (extended by BnGsim for a Population), segment_inbreeding.\n\nA pedigree is two vectors sire, dam of row numbers, 0 for an unknown parent, parents before their offspring.\nnrm(sire, dam[, ids]) is A (or its rows and columns ids), nrm_diag its diagonal 1 + F, and ainv its inverse, sparse.\ngrm(hap; p, loci) is VanRaden's (2008) first G, ZZ' / 2Σpq with Z = M - 2p for the dosages M of the haplotype pairs at loci.\nhinv(sire, dam, G, genotyped) is A⁻¹ + [0 0; 0 G⁻¹ - A₂₂⁻¹] for the genotyped rows.","title":"RelationshipMatrices (RelationshipsAdapter)"},{"category":"section","location":"upstream-contract/#Breeding-(BreedingAdapter)","page":"Upstream contract","text":"Names: MME, mme, RandomEffect, solve_mme, incidence, TM1997, feasible, rocs, pev_candidates, reml, MultiTrait, mt_stack, mt_blocks, mt_rinv.\n\nRandomEffect(Z, Kinv, σ²) takes the inverse of the covariance structure of the effect; mme(y, X, effects; σ²e) builds Henderson's equations and solve_mme returns the fixed effects, then the random ones, in one vector, equal to a dense solve of those equations.\nTM1997(u, sex, A, K) returns contributions c summing to 1, cAc, and a feasibility flag read by feasible.","title":"Breeding (BreedingAdapter)"},{"category":"section","location":"upstream-contract/#Contract-tests","page":"Upstream contract","text":"test/adapters.jl checks each convention above on small cases with exact answers: the haplotype layout and coordinates, the census across versions, the map of dense_haplotypes, ainv = inv(nrm), grm against the formula of VanRaden, hinv against its definition, the solution of mme against Henderson's equations solved densely, and TM1997's contributions. A new version of an upstream package that changes one of them fails these tests before it changes a simulation.","title":"Contract tests"},{"category":"section","location":"scenarios/#Scenarios","page":"Scenarios","text":"A scenario is a JSON file that describes a whole simulation: the species, the historical population and the founders sampled from it, the QTL and chip loci, the traits, the breeding scheme, the number of time steps, the master seed and the number of replicates. It is the input of the bngsim command line and of the planned web service, and runs in Julia with run_scenario.\n\nusing BnGsim\ns = read_scenario(\"examples/minimal.json\")   # read and validate\ndf = run_scenario(s)                         # per-step summaries of every replicate\n\nThe smallest example, examples/minimal.json:\n\n{\n  \"schema\": \"bngsim-scenario\",\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"name\": \"minimal\",\n  \"seed\": 2026,\n  \"steps\": 3,\n  \"species\": {\"name\": \"toy\", \"chromosome\": [20000000, 10000000], \"M\": 100000000},\n  \"founders\": {\"ne\": 100, \"nt\": 500, \"mr\": 1.0, \"nid\": 50},\n  \"loci\": {\"qtl\": {\"nlc\": 50, \"maf\": 0.01}},\n  \"traits\": [{\"name\": \"y\", \"h2\": 0.3}],\n  \"scheme\": {\"noff\": 50, \"nsire\": 5, \"ndam\": 20, \"evaluation\": \"phenotype\", \"selection\": \"truncation\"}\n}\n\n\n","title":"Scenarios"},{"category":"section","location":"scenarios/#Schema","page":"Scenarios","text":"The keys, their defaults and their meaning are listed in the docstring of Scenario (schema \"bngsim-scenario\", version 1). They mirror the Julia API: the scheme object takes the keywords of Scheme, with ASCII names for the Unicode ones (lambda, kappa, delta_F), and a trait takes those of BnGStructs.Trait (h2, sigma_a, mu, sex, age, and da for the distribution of the QTL effects).","title":"Schema"},{"category":"section","location":"scenarios/#Validation","page":"Scenarios","text":"read_scenario and parse_scenario check the whole document before anything runs and throw a ScenarioError that lists every problem with its path, rather than the first one; validate_scenario returns the list. Unknown keys, repeated keys, values of the wrong type and out-of-range values are errors, never ignored or repaired. So are combinations: a :gblup scheme without chip loci, weights that do not match the traits, a correlation matrix that is not a positive definite correlation matrix, more founders than the last historical generation holds, or a scheme that Scheme itself refuses. For example, a scheme with a misspelt key and a negative seed:\n\nusing BnGsim\ntext = replace(read(joinpath(pkgdir(BnGsim), \"examples\", \"minimal.json\"), String),\n    \"\\\"seed\\\": 2026\" => \"\\\"seed\\\": -1\", \"\\\"nsire\\\"\" => \"\\\"nsires\\\"\")\nvalidate_scenario(text)\n3-element Vector{String}:\n \"seed: must be an integer in 0:2^64-1; got -1\"\n \"scheme.nsires: unknown key (exp\"\u001b[93m\u001b[1m ⋯ 230 bytes ⋯ \u001b[22m\u001b[39m\"s, retain, report_reliability)\"\n \"scheme.nsire: required\"\n\nThe number of loci that pass the maf filters is only known once the founders are sampled; a panel larger than that fails then, with an ArgumentError.","title":"Validation"},{"category":"section","location":"scenarios/#Canonical-form","page":"Scenarios","text":"A Scenario holds every value, with the defaults filled. scenario_json and write_scenario write it with every key present, so the file states the whole simulation; reading it back gives an equal scenario, and writing that gives the same text.\n\ns = read_scenario(joinpath(pkgdir(BnGsim), \"examples\", \"minimal.json\"))\nprint(scenario_json(s))\n{\n  \"schema\": \"bngsim-scenario\",\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"name\": \"minimal\",\n  \"seed\": 2026,\n  \"replicates\": 1,\n  \"steps\": 3,\n  \"species\": {\n    \"name\": \"toy\",\n    \"chromosome\": [\n      20000000,\n      10000000\n    ],\n    \"morgan\": [\n      0.2,\n      0.1\n    ]\n  },\n  \"founders\": {\n    \"ne\": 100,\n    \"nt\": 500,\n    \"mr\": 1.0,\n    \"decline\": [],\n    \"nid\": 50,\n    \"shared_history\": true\n  },\n  \"loci\": {\n    \"qtl\": {\n      \"nlc\": 50,\n      \"maf\": 0.01\n    },\n    \"chip\": null,\n    \"overlap\": \"disjoint\"\n  },\n  \"traits\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"y\",\n      \"h2\": 0.3,\n      \"sigma_a\": 1.0,\n      \"mu\": 0.0,\n      \"sex\": 2,\n      \"age\": 0,\n      \"da\": {\n        \"family\": \"normal\",\n        \"mean\": 0.0,\n        \"sd\": 1.0\n      }\n    }\n  ],\n  \"genetic_correlation\": [\n    [\n      1.0\n    ]\n  ],\n  \"residual_correlation\": [\n    [\n      1.0\n    ]\n  ],\n  \"scheme\": {\n    \"noff\": 50,\n    \"nsire\": 5,\n    \"ndam\": 20,\n    \"evaluation\": \"phenotype\",\n    \"selection\": \"truncation\",\n    \"K\": null,\n    \"lambda\": 0.0,\n    \"kappa\": 0.0,\n    \"ub\": 1.0,\n    \"blend\": 0.05,\n    \"max_sire_age\": 0,\n    \"max_dam_age\": 0,\n    \"reml\": false,\n    \"weights\": [],\n    \"survival\": [],\n    \"cull_unselected\": false,\n    \"genotyped_cohorts\": 0,\n    \"ocs\": \"fixed\",\n    \"delta_F\": null,\n    \"record_meiosis\": false,\n    \"window\": 0,\n    \"ancestor_generations\": null,\n    \"retain\": \"all\",\n    \"report_reliability\": false\n  }\n}","title":"Canonical form"},{"category":"section","location":"scenarios/#Runs-and-reproducibility","page":"Scenarios","text":"run_replicate runs one replicate and returns its population and summary; run_scenario runs replicates 1:replicates and stacks their summaries with a column replicate. Every random draw comes from a SimKey of the scenario's seed: the historical population from replicate 0 when it is shared (founders.shared_history, the default), then for each replicate r the founders (Domain.FOUNDERS), the chip and QTL loci (Domain.GENOTYPING), the QTL effects (Domain.QTL) and the breeding loop. A replicate is therefore the same whether it runs alone or with others, and on any number of threads.","title":"Runs and reproducibility"},{"category":"section","location":"scenarios/#Limits-of-version-1","page":"Scenarios","text":"Founders come from a FisherWright historical population; a scenario takes no file paths (the service accepts no user paths, roadmap I9).\nTraits are additive (as the breeding loop).\nOne scheme for the whole run.\nProvenance is written by the command line with the outputs (roadmap I2), not by run_scenario.","title":"Limits of version 1"},{"category":"section","location":"relationships/#Relationships:-A,-G-and-realized-IBD","page":"Relationships: A, G and IBD","text":"This page compares four relationship matrices on one simulated population, and four measures of inbreeding:\n\nA, the numerator relationship matrix from the pedigree: the expected proportion of the genome shared identical by descent (IBD), twice the coancestry;\nI_mathrmseg, the realized IBD relationships: the proportion of the genome at which two animals carry the same founder haplotype, from the founder segments that the loop's crossover records determine (founder_segments, irm). It is exact: no loci are sampled;\nI_mathrmlocus, the same at the marker loci only;\nG, the genomic relationship matrix of VanRaden (2008), from the genotypes at the markers, centred on the founders' allele frequencies.\n\nEI_mathrmseg mid textpedigree = A: the pedigree gives the expectation, Mendelian sampling of whole chromosome segments the deviation. G estimates I_mathrmseg from identity by state, with a base given by the allele frequencies used for centring.","title":"Relationships: A, G and realized IBD"},{"category":"section","location":"relationships/#The-population","page":"Relationships: A, G and IBD","text":"Model, stated in full: diploid; 3 chromosomes of 40, 30 and 30 Mb with 1 cM/Mb (1 Morgan in all); meiosis with independent assortment and Poisson crossovers (Haldane, no interference); 100 founders with independent random haplotypes at 6000 loci (allele frequency ½, linkage equilibrium in expectation), unrelated and non-inbred by definition; an additive trait (h² = 0.3, QTL every 20th locus); 6 discrete generations of 100 offspring from 10 sires and 25 dams selected by truncation on PBLUP EBV. The scheme records the meioses.\n\nusing BnGsim, BnGStructs, RelationshipMatrices, DataFrames, Random, Statistics, LinearAlgebra\n\nsp  = GenericSpecies(\"tutorial\", [40_000_000, 30_000_000, 30_000_000])\nrng = Xoshiro(2026)\nvm  = VariantMap(sort(unique(rand(rng, 1:100_000_000, 6000))), sp)\nhap = Haplotype(BitMatrix(rand(rng, Bool, length(vm), 200)))\nmm  = MeiosisMap(sp, vm)\nt   = Trait(\"y\"; h² = 0.3)\nqtl = eqtl(hap, 1:20:length(vm), t; rng = rng)\npop = founder_population(hap, qtl, t, SimKey(1))\nsc  = Scheme(; noff = 100, nsire = 10, ndam = 25, evaluation = :pblup, record_meiosis = true)\nsummary = simulate!(pop, sc, mm, 6)\nsummary[:, [:generation, :mean, :F, :Fseg, :Fhet]]\n\u001b[1m7×5 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m generation \u001b[0m\u001b[1m mean        \u001b[0m\u001b[1m F         \u001b[0m\u001b[1m Fseg      \u001b[0m\u001b[1m Fhet      \u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m Int64      \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64     \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64   \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64   \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64   \u001b[0m\n─────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   1 │          0  3.28626e-16  0.0        0.0        0.005\n   2 │          1  0.670326     0.0        0.0        0.0205295\n   3 │          2  1.18591      0.025      0.015241   0.043677\n   4 │          3  1.73796      0.0446875  0.0475384  0.0788899\n   5 │          4  2.2064       0.0730469  0.122986   0.115658\n   6 │          5  2.37913      0.104766   0.130654   0.147632\n   7 │          6  2.65756      0.121611   0.157704   0.159408\n\nF is the mean pedigree inbreeding of each generation and Fseg the mean realized inbreeding from the founder segments; Fhet is the loss of heterozygosity at the loci (see generation_summary).","title":"The population"},{"category":"section","location":"relationships/#Four-relationship-matrices","page":"Relationships: A, G and IBD","text":"n    = length(pop)\nA    = RelationshipMatrices.nrm(pop.sire, pop.dam)\nsegs = founder_segments(pop, mm)\nIseg = irm(segs)\n\n# founder labels at the loci, from the segments\nlabels = zeros(UInt32, length(vm), 2n)\nfor h = 1:2n, k = segs.ptr[h]:segs.ptr[h+1]-1\n    r = searchsortedfirst(vm.pos, segs.start[k]):searchsortedlast(vm.pos, segs.stop[k])\n    labels[r, h] .= segs.label[k]\nend\nIlocus = irm(labels)\n\n# genotypes of all animals (`gt` is padded to whole words: take the loci);\n# G centred on the founders' allele frequencies\nnlc = length(vm)\nH = genomes(pop)\np = vec(sum(hap.gt[1:nlc, :]; dims = 2)) ./ hap.nhp\nG = RelationshipMatrices.grm(H; p = p)\n\nThe pairs of the last generation, by pedigree class:\n\nlast = collect(generation_ids(pop, 6))\ncls(i, j) = pop.sire[i] == pop.sire[j] && pop.dam[i] == pop.dam[j] ? \"full sibs\" :\n            pop.sire[i] == pop.sire[j] || pop.dam[i] == pop.dam[j] ? \"half sibs\" : \"other\"\npairs = [(i, j) for (a, i) in enumerate(last) for j in last[a+1:end]]\ndf = DataFrame(class = [cls(i, j) for (i, j) in pairs],\n    A = [A[i, j] for (i, j) in pairs], Iseg = [Iseg[i, j] for (i, j) in pairs],\n    Ilocus = [Ilocus[i, j] for (i, j) in pairs], G = [G[i, j] for (i, j) in pairs])\ncombine(groupby(df, :class), nrow => :pairs,\n    [c => (x -> round(mean(x); digits = 3)) => Symbol(c, \"_mean\") for c in (:A, :Iseg, :G)]...,\n    [c => (x -> round(std(x); digits = 3)) => Symbol(c, \"_sd\") for c in (:A, :Iseg, :G)]...)\n\u001b[1m3×8 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m class     \u001b[0m\u001b[1m pairs \u001b[0m\u001b[1m A_mean  \u001b[0m\u001b[1m Iseg_mean \u001b[0m\u001b[1m G_mean  \u001b[0m\u001b[1m A_sd    \u001b[0m\u001b[1m Iseg_sd \u001b[0m\u001b[1m G_sd    \u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m String    \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Int64 \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64 \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64   \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64 \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64 \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64 \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64 \u001b[0m\n─────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   1 │ half sibs    576    0.46       0.515    0.503    0.042    0.187    0.19\n   2 │ other       4362    0.238      0.286    0.273    0.046    0.159    0.161\n   3 │ full sibs     12    0.689      0.73     0.726    0.074    0.205    0.205\n\nWithin a class the pedigree assigns nearly the same A to every pair; the realized relationships spread around it, several times more. Under Haldane's map the spread of half-sib and full-sib relationships has an exact value, which the loop reproduces (validation/ibd.jl): it depends on the genome's length in Morgans, here only 1 Morgan, so the spread is large. G spreads only slightly more than I_mathrmseg: with 6000 loci, the sampling of loci and identity by state add little.\n\nThe realized relationships are also higher than A on average. That is selection, not chance: over 20 replicates of this scheme the mean of I_mathrmseg - A over the pairs of the last generation is +0.033 (s.e. 0.008) under truncation on PBLUP EBV and −0.004 (s.e. 0.004) under random selection (validation/ibd-selection.jl). Selected parents carry favourable QTL alleles, so their descendants share the segments around them more often than Mendelian sampling implies; A assumes Mendelian sampling and ignores this. This replicate lies within the spread of the replicates under selection (s.d. 0.034).\n\nHow the matrices relate over all pairs of the last generation:\n\noff = [(i, j) for (a, i) in enumerate(last) for j in last[a+1:end]]\nv(M) = [M[i, j] for (i, j) in off]\nslope(x, y) = cov(x, y) / var(x)\nDataFrame(comparison = [\"Iseg on A\", \"Ilocus on Iseg\", \"G on Iseg\"],\n    correlation = round.([cor(v(A), v(Iseg)), cor(v(Iseg), v(Ilocus)), cor(v(Iseg), v(G))]; digits = 3),\n    slope = round.([slope(v(A), v(Iseg)), slope(v(Iseg), v(Ilocus)), slope(v(Iseg), v(G))]; digits = 3),\n    max_abs_difference = round.([maximum(abs, v(Iseg) .- v(A)), maximum(abs, v(Ilocus) .- v(Iseg)),\n        maximum(abs, v(G) .- v(Iseg))]; digits = 3))\n\u001b[1m3×4 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m comparison     \u001b[0m\u001b[1m correlation \u001b[0m\u001b[1m slope   \u001b[0m\u001b[1m max_abs_difference \u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m String         \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64     \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64 \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64            \u001b[0m\n─────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   1 │ Iseg on A             0.492    1.013               0.664\n   2 │ Ilocus on Iseg        1.0      0.997               0.015\n   3 │ G on Iseg             0.997    1.008               0.067\n\nI_mathrmlocus differs from I_mathrmseg only by sampling 6000 positions of the genome. G follows I_mathrmseg with a regression slope near one because it is centred on the founders' frequencies, the base of the pedigree; centred on the frequencies of the current generation it would be shifted down by the coancestry accumulated since the founders.","title":"Four relationship matrices"},{"category":"section","location":"relationships/#Inbreeding","page":"Relationships: A, G and IBD","text":"Fped = diag(A)[last] .- 1\nFseg = segment_inbreeding(segs; ids = last)\nchrs = [mm.first[c]:mm.last[c] for c in eachindex(mm.first)]\nFroh = froh(H.gt[1:nlc, vcat([[2i - 1, 2i] for i in last]...)], vm.pos, chrs; min_len = 4_000_000)\nFseg4 = segment_inbreeding(segs; ids = last, min_len = 4_000_000)\nFG = diag(G)[last] .- 1\nDataFrame(measure = [\"pedigree F\", \"realized F (segments)\", \"realized F, runs ≥ 4 Mb\",\n        \"F_ROH, runs ≥ 4 Mb\", \"diag(G) − 1\"],\n    mean = round.(mean.([Fped, Fseg, Fseg4, Froh, FG]); digits = 4),\n    sd = round.(std.([Fped, Fseg, Fseg4, Froh, FG]); digits = 4),\n    cor_with_Fseg = round.([cor(Fped, Fseg), 1.0, cor(Fseg4, Fseg), cor(Froh, Fseg), cor(FG, Fseg)]; digits = 3))\n\u001b[1m5×4 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m measure                 \u001b[0m\u001b[1m mean    \u001b[0m\u001b[1m sd      \u001b[0m\u001b[1m cor_with_Fseg \u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m String                  \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64 \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64 \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64       \u001b[0m\n─────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n   1 │ pedigree F                0.1216   0.0467          0.385\n   2 │ realized F (segments)     0.1577   0.1487          1.0\n   3 │ realized F, runs ≥ 4 Mb   0.1465   0.1495          0.992\n   4 │ F_ROH, runs ≥ 4 Mb        0.1465   0.1495          0.992\n   5 │ diag(G) − 1               0.1513   0.1512          0.997\n\nRealized inbreeding varies among animals with the same pedigree inbreeding, for the same reason as the relationships, so the pedigree F explains little of it. It is higher than the pedigree F on average, again because of selection (+0.015, s.e. 0.004, over the 20 replicates; +0.001, s.e. 0.003, without selection). Runs of homozygosity of at least 4 Mb (4 cM) estimate the autozygosity in IBD runs of that length, the recent inbreeding; they miss shorter, older autozygous segments. With a marker every 17 kb on average, a run differs from its autozygous segment by a few marker intervals, so here F_mathrmROH and the realized F in runs of at least 4 Mb agree to the precision shown. The diagonal of G, centred on the founders' frequencies, follows the realized inbreeding closely with dense markers.","title":"Inbreeding"},{"category":"section","location":"relationships/#Reproducing","page":"Relationships: A, G and IBD","text":"Every random draw is keyed by SimKey(1), so the page gives the same numbers on any machine and number of threads (for a given set of package versions). The variance check of realized relationships against Haldane's map is validation/ibd.jl; segment IBD is validated against A and locus-level IBD in RelationshipMatrices.","title":"Reproducing"},{"category":"section","location":"cli/#Command-line","page":"Command line","text":"bin/bngsim runs a scenario from the shell. It is the interface the planned web service will call, so its arguments, output and exit codes are versioned (CLI interface 1) separately from the package.\n\nbngsim validate SCENARIO.json\nbngsim estimate SCENARIO.json\nbngsim run SCENARIO.json --out DIR [--replicates A:B] [--memory-limit SIZE] [--checkpoint-every K]\n           [--archive none|chip|qtl|all]\nbngsim resume DIR\nbngsim merge DIR... --out DIR\nbngsim version [--json]\n\nThe script starts Julia in the environment of the checkout (or BNGSIM_PROJECT) with BNGSIM_THREADS threads (default: all) and calls cli. From Julia, BnGsim.cli([\"run\", \"s.json\", \"--out\", \"out\"]) does the same and returns the exit code.","title":"Command line"},{"category":"section","location":"cli/#Output","page":"Command line","text":"Every command writes one JSON object to stdout and diagnostics to stderr, so a caller reads the result from stdout and the reason for a failure from both.\n\nvalidate prints {\"status\": \"valid\", \"scenario_sha256\", ...}, or {\"status\": \"invalid\", \"errors\": [...]} with every problem of the scenario and its path.\nestimate prints the expected peak memory, a conservative bound, the disk written and a rough run time (Resource estimate).\nrun writes three files to DIR, which must not exist or must be empty (results are never overwritten):\nscenario.json, the canonical scenario (scenario_json);\nsummary.csv, the per-step summaries of every replicate (run_scenario; columns in generation_summary and step!, among them the QTL at which the favourable allele is segregating, fixed or lost, the realized selection intensity of each sex, genomic inbreeding Fgrm and, with report_reliability, the candidates' mean reliability), numbers in Julia's shortest round-trip form;\naggregate.csv, per time step over the replicates, the mean, standard deviation and standard error of every summary, and of quantities derived within each replicate: genetic gain dG, rates of inbreeding dF (pedigree) and dFhet (loss of heterozygosity), and the variance and genic variance relative to the first step, var_ratio and genic_ratio (aggregate);\nprovenance.json: the canonical scenario and its SHA-256, the seed and replicates, the BnGsim, CLI and schema versions, the Julia and package versions, the SHA-256 of the active manifest, the operating system, CPU, host and threads, the start and end times, and the SHA-256 of the other two files.\nIt then prints {\"status\": \"completed\", \"outputs\": [...], ...}. With --memory-limit SIZE (bytes, or a number with K, M, G or T), a scenario whose memory bound exceeds SIZE is refused before it runs.\nWith --archive PANEL, run also writes an archive of each replicate (pedigree, records, true and estimated breeding values, QTL, and PLINK genotypes at the loci of PANEL) to DIR/archive/r<N>/.\nresume DIR continues a run that was stopped or killed (below).\nversion --json prints the versions and the manifest hash.\n\nscenario.json and summary.csv depend only on the scenario, the replicates, the Julia and package versions, and the compiler flags. They are byte-identical whatever the number of threads: for examples/minimal.json, checked at 1 and 8 threads. Flags that change the compiled code change the last bits of floating-point sums: Julia started with --check-bounds=yes (as Pkg.test does) gives other last digits than the default. Everything about the run itself is in provenance.json.","title":"Output"},{"category":"section","location":"cli/#Replicates-on-several-hosts","page":"Command line","text":"bngsim run ... --replicates A:B runs part of the replicates of a scenario, for example on several hosts, and bngsim merge DIR... --out OUT combines completed runs of the same scenario (the SHA-256 of their scenarios must agree) whose replicates do not overlap. OUT gets the scenario, the summaries ordered by replicate, the aggregate and a provenance that lists the runs merged. Since every replicate is keyed by its number, the merged summary.csv and aggregate.csv are byte for byte those of a single run of all the replicates, in whatever order the runs are merged or finished.","title":"Replicates on several hosts"},{"category":"section","location":"cli/#Stopping-and-resuming","page":"Command line","text":"While a run is unfinished its state lives in DIR/.bngsim-run/: the scenario, the shared historical population, the summaries of the replicates done, and a checkpoint of the one in progress. A file DIR/CANCEL (created by a user or the service) stops the run after the current time step: the state is saved and bngsim exits with code 7. SIGINT (Ctrl-C) and SIGTERM (as a job scheduler or kill sends it) to bin/bngsim do the same: the launcher creates DIR/CANCEL, so the run stops at the next step boundary, never inside a step. Before the run has saved any state (while Julia loads, or the scenario is checked) there is nothing to keep, and the launcher stops Julia at once; a second signal also stops it at once, with the last saved state kept (state files are written atomically, never half-written). The signals are handled by the launcher, not by Julia, so that they behave the same on every Julia version and number of threads. bngsim resume DIR removes DIR/CANCEL and continues. With --checkpoint-every K the state is also saved every K steps, so that a process that is killed, or a host that goes down, loses at most K steps.\n\nA resumed run gives the same scenario.json and summary.csv, byte for byte, as one that was never interrupted, since every random draw is keyed by its coordinate (replicate, step, animal) and nothing else carries over between steps. provenance.json lists each session (start, end, host, CPU, threads, and whether it was stopped or completed). run refuses a directory that holds an unfinished run. The state uses Julia's serialization, so it resumes only under the same BnGsim and Julia versions; it is for continuing a run, not for archiving.","title":"Stopping and resuming"},{"category":"section","location":"cli/#Exit-codes","page":"Command line","text":"Code Meaning\n0 success\n2 usage: unknown command, missing or invalid arguments\n3 the scenario is missing, unreadable or does not validate\n4 refused: the output directory is not empty or holds an unfinished run, the replicates are outside the scenario's, or there is nothing to resume (or its state is from another version)\n5 refused before running: the memory bound exceeds --memory-limit\n6 the simulation stopped with an explicit error, e.g. fewer segregating loci than the panels ask for\n7 stopped at a step boundary by DIR/CANCEL (also created by bin/bngsim on SIGINT or SIGTERM); bngsim resume DIR continues\n70 internal error (a bug)\n\nA run that fails before it saved any state, or is refused, writes no output directory (ExitCode).","title":"Exit codes"},{"category":"section","location":"cli/#Start-up-time","page":"Command line","text":"The scenario and command-line paths are compiled into the package image when BnGsim is precompiled (a workload of two small scenarios), which takes about half a minute once per installation. After that, bngsim run examples/minimal.json takes about 3 s from the shell, start-up included, instead of about 35 s.","title":"Start-up time"}]
}
